OAI_PAPER({"slug": "Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026", "title": "Purely cosmetic surgery on knots in the three-sphere", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Purely-Cosmetic-Surgery-on-Knots-in-the-Three-Sphere-September-23-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "306", "family_title": "The purely cosmetic surgery conjecture", "abstract_html": "We prove the purely cosmetic surgery conjecture for knots in the three-sphere. Distinct Dehn surgery slopes on a nontrivial smooth knot never give orientation-preservingly homeomorphic manifolds.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "For \\(r,s\\in\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\), an orientation-preserving homeomorphism \\( S^{3}_{r}(K)\\cong S^{3}_{s}(K) \\) implies \\(r=s\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K\\subset S^{3}\\) be a smooth nontrivial knot, with exterior \\(E_K=S^{3}\\setminus\\operatorname{int}(\\nu K)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The meridian \\(\\mu\\) and preferred longitude \\(\\lambda\\) identify unoriented slopes with \\(\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\): the primitive class \\(p\\mu+q\\lambda\\), up to simultaneous sign, represents \\(p/q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(S^{3}_{r}(K)\\) for the filling of slope \\(r\\), oriented by extension of the orientation of \\(E_K\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(r,s\\in\\mathbb Q\\cup\\{\\infty\\}\\), an orientation-preserving homeomorphism\n\\[\nS^{3}_{r}(K)\\cong S^{3}_{s}(K)\n\\]\nimplies \\(r=s\\). The homeomorphism is not required to preserve a surgery core or the knot exterior.</p>", "proof": "<p>Assume distinct purely cosmetic slopes exist. Proposition 1 reduces them to \\(-2,2\\) on a genus-two knot and supplies an arbitrary oriented diffeomorphism \\(\\varphi:Y_{-2}\\to Y_2\\).</p><p>Propositions 2–4 construct the ordinary units and the signed negative interval map with its local determinant comparison. Propositions 5 and 9 give fixed caps with a nonzero ordinary pairing. Propositions 6–8 supply the lift bookkeeping, simultaneous metric family, and Abelian inequalities. Choose the outside delays and exceptional evaluations as in Proposition 12, then apply Proposition 10 to obtain a finite stack with\n\\[\n\\Omega\\ne0,\\qquad n_D^{\\rm tot}&gt;0.\n\\]</p><p>Proposition 11 supplies the lens correction table. Propositions 12–15 establish the numerical and analytic exclusions, leaving only legitimate instanton links and single minimum trace lens ends. Proposition 16 constructs exhaustive regular collars at those lens ends. Proposition 17 applies the weighted boundary rule and the same ordinary determinant weights to obtain\n\\[\n0=\\sigma_0\\,2^{\\,n_D^{\\rm tot}-1}\\Omega,\n\\]\ncontradicting \\(\\Omega\\ne0\\) over \\(\\mathbb Q\\).</p><p>Thus the remaining pair is impossible. The meridional and zero cases were already excluded in Proposition 1. Therefore orientation-preservingly homeomorphic fillings of a smooth nontrivial knot in \\(S^3\\) have equal slopes.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "Proposition 1.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "If a smooth nontrivial knot in \\(S^{3}\\) has distinct purely cosmetic slopes, then its genus is two and the slopes are \\(-2\\) and \\(2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the knot and slope conventions of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">A pair of distinct slopes is purely cosmetic if its fillings are orientation-preservingly homeomorphic.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If a smooth nontrivial knot in \\(S^{3}\\) has distinct purely cosmetic slopes, then its genus is two and the slopes are \\(-2\\) and \\(2\\). In this remaining case an oriented homeomorphism may be replaced by an oriented diffeomorphism\n\\[\n\\varphi:Y_{-2}\\longrightarrow Y_{2},\n\\qquad\nY_j=S^{3}_{j}(K),\\quad Y_\\infty=S^{3}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Gordon–Luecke exclude a second filling giving \\(S^{3}\\), and therefore settle the meridional case. For finite slopes,\n\\[\nH_1(S^{3}_{p/q}(K);\\mathbb Z)\\cong\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbb Z/|p|,&amp;p\\ne0,\\\\\n\\mathbb Z,&amp;p=0.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nPrimitivity makes \\(0/1\\) the unique slope with zero numerator.</p><p>The restrictions of Ni–Wu and Hanselman, together with the exclusion of the pairs \\(\\pm1/q\\) by Daemi–Lidman–Miller Eismeier, leave only \\(\\{-2,2\\}\\) on a genus-two knot. This is the consequence of their results used in the paper. The three-dimensional smoothing theorem replaces the remaining homeomorphism by a homotopic diffeomorphism; homotopy preserves degree and hence orientation.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Convention 2.3; Lemmas 2.2, 2.4, 2.8–2.12; Lemma 4.7; Lemmas 9.2–9.3; Proposition 9.5, including its ordinary analytic prerequisites", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The irreducible ordinary instanton complexes, coherent integral orientations, compatible perturbations, exact-support representatives, compactifications in the required dimensions, and regular gluing constructions used …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work with determinant-one gauge transformations, fixed determinant connections, and fixed determinant-preserving transition identifications.</p><p class=\"li\">On \\(Y_0\\), the determinant evaluates oddly on the capped Seifert surface \\(A\\). On \\(Y_{\\pm1},Y_{\\pm2},Y_\\infty\\), it is trivializable.</p><p class=\"li\">On a completed cobordism, \\(i\\) denotes the unframed ASD index with boundary representatives fixed. Gauges may approach independent parallel stabilizers at different ends; normal remainders have small positive exponential weights.</p><p class=\"li\">A match at an isolated flat adds \\(h^0\\). At \\(S^1\\times S^2\\), harmonic flat parameters are retained before conjugation: varying an asymptote adds \\(h^1\\), and matching adds \\(h^0+h^1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A particle is an ideal curvature-concentration point carrying positive integral charge.</p><p class=\"li\">All families considered are the finite ordinary families needed below, with product-compatible face data and equation perturbations independent of incidence marks.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The irreducible ordinary instanton complexes, coherent integral orientations, compatible perturbations, exact-support representatives, compactifications in the required dimensions, and regular gluing constructions used below can be chosen simultaneously. This ordinary library is independent of the later nonvanishing count and of the spinor exclusion argument. Prescribed harmonic reducers can be preserved while their normal derivatives are regularized.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 supplies the ordinary complexes, and Lemma 2.2 fixes charge and actual-index bookkeeping. Lemmas 2.3–2.7 construct compatible equations and exact representatives, including their zero-evaluation avoidances and low-dimensional incidence budgets. Lemmas 2.8–2.10 provide the required regular gluing charts.</p><p>Choose the usual integral determinant-line orientation systems with true-end orientations, homology orientations, and integral determinant lifts fixed. They are orientable for the determinant-one problem. Reference connection spaces in a fixed relative sector are affine and connected, so an excision comparison extends with constant relative sign; associative gluing preserves it. No part of this construction assumes the later nonzero count.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 2.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every reducible flat at a rational end is central and has \\( (h^0,h^1)=(3,0). \\) The odd determinant on \\(Y_0\\) admits no reducible projectively flat connection.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider the ordinary ends and determinants specified in Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">At a trivializable determinant end, use a square-root normalization.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every reducible flat at a rational end is central and has\n\\[\n(h^0,h^1)=(3,0).\n\\]\nThe odd determinant on \\(Y_0\\) admits no reducible projectively flat connection. Sufficiently small perturbations fixing the central connections preserve these properties. The irreducible-only ordinary complex is consequently available; take\n\\[\nC(Y_\\infty)=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The first homology of each rational end is zero or \\(\\mathbb Z/2\\). A reducible \\(SU(2)\\) representation factors through first homology and takes values in \\(\\{1,-1\\}\\). Its adjoint system is therefore trivial, giving \\(h^0=3\\), \\(h^1=0\\). The two central lifts at an order-two end remain distinct fixed-determinant connections.</p><p>A reducible projectively flat connection on \\(Y_0\\) would split into lines with equal real first Chern classes, forcing even determinant evaluation on \\(A\\). This contradicts admissibility. At a central rational flat the Coulomb Hessian is invertible. The implicit function theorem preserves its isolation, and three-dimensional compactness excludes newly appearing reducers for perturbations tending to zero. Choose the unperturbed flat problem on \\(S^3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Increasing relative charge by one increases \\(i\\) by eight.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(\\mathcal E=-p_1(\\operatorname{ad}E)/4\\), interpreted by Chern–Weil on the completed connection.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed-limit index convention of Proposition 2.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Increasing relative charge by one increases \\(i\\) by eight. For a connected piece with \\(b_1=0\\) and only central rational ends,\n\\[\ni=8\\mathcal E-3(1+b^+).\n\\]\nFor replacements with identical boundary flats and transitions, including matching,\n\\[\n\\Delta i=8\\Delta\\mathcal E-\\frac32\\Delta(\\chi+\\sigma).\n\\]\nThe discrete nonnegative lower charge bounds used below persist under sufficiently small perturbations. A regular count over a \\(p\\)-dimensional metric family has \\(i=-p\\); its one-dimensional boundary relation has \\(i=1-p\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The ASD symbol contributes \\(-3(\\chi+\\sigma)/2\\), and the bundle term is \\(-2p_1(\\operatorname{ad}E)=8\\mathcal E\\). The scalar fixed-limit complex at central ends has index \\(-1-b^+\\); tensoring with the parallel adjoint fiber gives the displayed formula, including index \\(-3\\) for a central flat cylinder. Excision cancels identical boundary terms.</p><p>Nonnegative charge for ASD fields, discreteness of the permitted charge set, and compactness preserve the lower bounds for sufficiently small perturbations. On a gradient neck,\n\\[\n\\int_a^b\\|\\dot a(t)\\|^2dt\n=\\operatorname{CS}(a(a))+h(a(a))\n-\\operatorname{CS}(a(b))-h(a(b)).\n\\]\nThe perturbation error is bounded by \\(2\\|h\\|_\\infty\\), independently of length; intermediate functional values telescope. An irreducible nonconstant trajectory has index at least one before translation quotient. A charge-\\(q\\) particle lowers field index by \\(8q\\) and supplies at most \\(4q\\) location parameters.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "There is a smooth conjugation-equivariant operation \\(g\\mapsto\\widehat g\\), homotopic to the identity relative to endpoints, which fixes commuting paths of nonzero winding and sends every commuting zero-winding path …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(1/2&lt;b&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider the Hilbert manifold of \\(H^b\\) paths \\(g:[0,1]\\to SU(2)\\) with endpoints \\(1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth conjugation-equivariant operation \\(g\\mapsto\\widehat g\\), homotopic to the identity relative to endpoints, which fixes commuting paths of nonzero winding and sends every commuting zero-winding path uniformly away from \\(-1\\). It uses only the given path.</p>", "proof": "<p>Identify \\(SU(2)\\) with unit quaternions. For a nonconstant commuting path,\n\\[\nM(g)=\\int_0^1\\operatorname{Im}g(t)\\otimes\\operatorname{Im}g(t)\\,dt\n\\]\nhas rank one and a positive simple eigenvalue. Its eigenspace defines a smooth unoriented line \\(L(g)\\) nearby. Projection to \\(\\mathbb R\\oplus L(g)\\), followed by normalization, gives a circle path \\(h_g\\). In a zero-winding neighborhood it has a unique logarithm \\(s_g\\) with both endpoint values zero.</p><p>The set \\(C\\) of commuting zero-winding paths meeting \\(\\{\\operatorname{Re}\\le0\\}\\) is closed. Choose an invariant neighborhood \\(U\\) containing \\(C\\), containing no commuting nonzero-winding path, and supporting the preceding construction. A smooth invariant function \\(f\\), equal to one on \\(C\\) and supported in \\(U\\), gives\n\\[\n\\widehat g(t)=g(t)\\exp(-f(g)s_g(t)).\n\\]\nMultiplying the exponent by a homotopy parameter gives the required homotopy. On \\(C\\) the result is constant. Other commuting zero-winding paths remain in the positive-real hemisphere under partial cancellation. Nonzero-winding commuting paths are unchanged. Smoothness follows from \\(H^b\\hookrightarrow C^0\\) and the Banach algebra property.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 9.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At a true \\(J\\)-face, the sphere event is the disjoint union of the events on the two half-sphere sweeps, with prescribed signs.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Apply Lemma 2.3 to normalized sphere holonomy sweeps.</p><p class=\"li\">The primary cut \\(J\\cong S^3\\) separates the two simply connected trace halves.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At a true \\(J\\)-face, the sphere event is the disjoint union of the events on the two half-sphere sweeps, with prescribed signs. During switching, a commuting path whose two half degrees vanish retains a uniform avoidance gap. At the face every event is intrinsic to its assigned side, with no transport derivative along the growing neck.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Hurewicz identification on the simply connected traces gives a homotopy from the whole sphere map to the signed concatenation of its half maps. Insert identity intervals and fixed whiskers. At concatenation, the zero-degree logarithms satisfy \\(s=s_1+s_2\\). Interpolate the modifying multipliers by\n\\[\n\\exp(-(1-u)fs)\\exp(-uf_1s_1)\\exp(-uf_2s_2).\n\\]\nA segment meeting the nonpositive hemisphere has both relevant cutoffs equal to one, so its angle is cancelled. A segment in the positive hemisphere stays between its original angle and zero.</p><p>Keep event marks off identity intervals. Since the event value \\(-1\\) and the modification are conjugation invariant, whisker conjugations may be removed in favor of intrinsic half frames. During finite switches retain the closed two-dimensional homotopy-support charts and their limiting unions; near the true face retain only intrinsic compact half supports. This preserves closed incidence conditions without thickening supports.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.5", "orig": "Lemma 2.8; ordinary part of Lemma 9.4, Lemma 9.7, and Proposition 9.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Arbitrarily small compatible perturbations make irreducible equations and any specified finite collection of evaluations transverse.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use compact based loop-and-path samples, localized smooth outputs, and small-ball curvature cutoffs.</p><p class=\"li\">On faces, every connected sampling graph and its cutoff balls belong to one component; incompatible crossing terms are turned off.</p><p class=\"li\">Use weighted summable coefficients in a countable library.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Arbitrarily small compatible perturbations make irreducible equations and any specified finite collection of evaluations transverse. Different split components have independent variations. At a harmonic reducer, perturbations may vanish in value and prescribe arbitrary finite-rank complex-linear kernel-to-cokernel maps in the off-diagonal operator. Evaluation at an \\(S^1\\times S^2\\) end is transverse to a local flat slice; fixing its central flat imposes three real conditions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Finite holonomies detect an irreducible stabilizer and any specified finite-dimensional horizontal tangent space. If all loop variations are one simultaneous infinitesimal conjugation, transporting that conjugating element defines an infinitesimal gauge transformation. Slice functions and localized outputs therefore supply all finite-dimensional equation and cut directions.</p><p>For universal surjectivity use \\(W^{1,q}_{\\delta_S}\\to L^q_{\\delta_S}\\), \\(q&gt;4\\), with \\(0&lt;\\delta_S&lt;\\delta_H\\) in one root-free interval. First kill cut cokernel components by independent value variations. Smooth compact outputs, avoiding finitely many particle positions, are dense in the equation target and detect its remaining cokernel. No smoothness of distributional graph adjoints is asserted. Primal elliptic bootstrap and the root-free end estimate transfer surjectivity to higher little-Hölder orders and weight \\(\\delta_H\\).</p><p>At a reducer, torus-equivariant slice-linear outputs vanish on commuting data and realize the required complex-linear normal maps. Extend a harmonic asymptotic variation by a cutoff and cancel its compact equation error using interior variations. At a central \\(S^1\\times S^2\\) flat the slice is \\(h\\vartheta\\), \\(h\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2)\\), before conjugation, so the central condition has codimension three.</p><p>Choose library weights dominating the countable smooth operator and exponential end seminorms. Relative parameter cutoffs preserve faces. Moving samples lose derivatives, but compatible implicit charts at successively higher orders provide every finite differentiability order needed for Sard–Smale.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.6", "orig": "Lemma 2.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These perturbations make the required irreducible critical points and trajectories regular.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">At actual Floer ends use gradients of small time-independent invariant thickened-holonomy functions.</p><p class=\"li\">The perturbations fix central critical points.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These perturbations make the required irreducible critical points and trajectories regular. Their gradients are bounded, and their Hessians along compact smooth configuration arcs are bounded on \\(L^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Average simultaneous based holonomies over submersively thickened paths and a disk of bases. Cauchy–Schwarz gives the \\(L^2\\) Hessian bound; thin-loop variations are limits of these averages.</p><p>For uniqueness of trajectories, their difference obeys\n\\[\n\\dot\\xi+D\\xi=B(t)\\xi\n\\]\nwith \\(D\\) symmetric and \\(B(t)\\) bounded. For \\(m=\\langle D\\xi,\\xi\\rangle/\\|\\xi\\|^2\\),\n\\[\nm'\\le\\tfrac12\\|B\\|^2,\\qquad\n(\\log\\|\\xi\\|^2)'=-2m+O(\\|B\\|).\n\\]\nApply this in both time directions. An irreducible trajectory consequently has irreducible slices, nonzero velocity, and no repetition modulo gauge; finite energy excludes repetition.</p><p>A universal cokernel field satisfies\n\\[\nd_a^*\\eta=0,\\qquad\n-\\dot\\eta+H_a\\eta=0.\n\\]\nFunctions vanishing on a short isolated trajectory arc, with arbitrary normal differential, force \\(\\eta=c(t)\\dot a\\). Functions along that arc force \\(c\\) constant. Differentiating the trajectory equation then shows that a nonzero \\(c\\) makes the velocity norm constant, contradicting finite energy. Thus the universal cokernel vanishes. Sard–Smale and relative extensions finish the construction.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.7", "orig": "Lemma 4.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "These exact tests have degrees two for a moving surface point and four for a fixed point, and represent \\(4\\mu\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Surface occurrences are separately labelled; their representatives have pairwise transverse intersections and no triple intersections.</p><p class=\"li\">Fixed point probes avoid surfaces.</p><p class=\"li\">On cylindrical ends, the labelled support loops are disjoint.</p><p class=\"li\">At an evaluation point use the rank-at-most-one locus of two sections of the universal complexified adjoint bundle of rank three.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>These exact tests have degrees two for a moving surface point and four for a fixed point, and represent \\(4\\mu\\). Their zero- and one-dimensional ordinary compactifications have no particles and only ordinary irreducible breaks. The assertion includes representative-comparison parameters.</p>", "proof": "<p>The principal rank-drop locus has complex codimension two and represents\n\\[\nc_2(\\operatorname{ad}E\\otimes\\mathbb C)=-p_1(\\operatorname{ad}E)=4\\mu\n\\]\nafter slant product. The rank-zero locus has higher codimension.</p><p>Sample cutoffs include atom contributions\n\\[\n\\int\\rho|F_{a_0}|^2+8\\pi^2\\sum_j\\ell_j\\rho(p_j).\n\\]\nA graph meeting an atom is disabled; detecting graphs avoiding the finite atom set still provide independent values. Thus an exact test persists unless its evaluation point hits an atom, although its residual formula may depend on retained particle positions.</p><p>For one particle, the sums of released degree and position dimension are respectively\n\\[\n0+4,\\quad2+2,\\quad4+0,\\quad4+0\n\\]\noutside supports, on one surface, at two transverse surfaces, or at a fixed point. If charge \\(\\ell\\) is carried by \\(r\\le\\ell\\) particles, the residual dimension is at most\n\\[\nd-8\\ell+4r\\le d-4\\ell&lt;0\n\\]\nfor \\(d\\le1\\). The same applies to extracted levels. An atom escaping through a constant region can discard at most one disjoint cylindrical label. A constant critical cylinder cannot carry a generic mark: after translation its mark has only one loop coordinate against codimension four. The remaining breaks have the prescribed product representatives and coherent orientations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.8", "orig": "Lemma 2.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Ordinary gluing gives exhaustive local product charts, including variable neck lengths.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Completed ordinary pieces have matching isolated nondegenerate irreducible limits, fixed determinant transitions, and compatible cuts.</p><p class=\"li\">The matched cut problem is regular.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Ordinary gluing gives exhaustive local product charts, including variable neck lengths. An isolated regular match gives exactly one nearby solution for every sufficiently long prescribed neck, with determinant-line gluing orientation.</p>", "proof": "<p>The tangential operator has \\(h^0=h^1=0\\), hence a spectral gap and exponential decay. Pregluing has exponentially small equation and cut errors. Splice half right inverses on the complement of the matched tangent space. Long cutoff transitions make commutators small; a Neumann series gives the inverse, and the contraction theorem gives the local chart. Exponential estimates put every converging solution into its uniqueness neighborhood.</p><p>The center \\(\\{\\pm1\\}\\) acts trivially on adjoint data and extends across either piece, so it supplies no second match. Only determinant-preserving matching isomorphisms are allowed. Pairing adjacent end orientation lines in cobordism order gives the coherent orientation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.9", "orig": "Lemma 2.11", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a sufficiently small-energy neck, modulo gauge, \\( a(t)=\\alpha+h(t)\\vartheta+b(t),\\qquad b\\perp H^1_\\alpha,\\quad d_\\alpha^*b=0. \\) The temporal parallel part is zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Give \\(L=S^1\\times S^2\\) its product metric.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\alpha\\) be flat, with parallel stabilizer \\(H\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a sufficiently small-energy neck, modulo gauge,\n\\[\na(t)=\\alpha+h(t)\\vartheta+b(t),\\qquad\nb\\perp H^1_\\alpha,\\quad d_\\alpha^*b=0.\n\\]\nThe temporal parallel part is zero. For sufficiently small \\(\\delta&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\|b(t)\\|_k\\le C_k\\!\\left(B_-e^{-\\delta(t+T)}\n+B_+e^{-\\delta(T-t)}\\right)\n+C_k\\int_{-T}^Te^{-\\delta|t-s|}\\rho_k(s)\\,ds.\n\\]\nThe derivative of \\(h\\) has the same normal-size and error bound, giving length-independent total drift and exponential closeness on middle strips to one middle flat. At a central flat \\(h\\) has three real coordinates. Lens ends have the same conclusions without harmonic spatial modes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every \\(\\alpha+h\\vartheta\\) is flat because \\(\\vartheta\\wedge\\vartheta=0\\). Parallel rotations remove the temporal parallel part. The slice equation solves for the remaining temporal component on the complement of constants. The projected normal equation is\n\\[\n(\\partial_t+K_\\alpha)b=R,\\qquad\nK_\\alpha=*d_\\alpha,\\quad\n\\|R\\|_k\\le C_k\\epsilon\\|b\\|_k+C_k\\rho_k.\n\\]\nThe normal operator has a spectral gap. Propagate positive modes from the left and negative modes from the right, then absorb the small nonlinear term. Integrating the harmonic derivative gives no factor of \\(T\\).</p><p>Starting near a middle cluster flat, the same estimates keep a maximal slice interval inside a slightly larger slice until a strip leaves the small-energy regime; decreasing the energy threshold excludes that exit. Non-small stretches yield levels or particles. Chern–Simons is constant on the connected flat space of \\(L\\), so every nonflat level costs positive integral charge. After extracting finitely many losses, the estimates apply throughout. Smooth strip convergence gives uniform holonomy convergence on compact bounded path families.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.10", "orig": "Lemma 2.12", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every match gives exactly one nearby solution for sufficiently long necks, and every converging solution is obtained this way.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Two completed ordinary pieces meet at an auxiliary flat.</p><p class=\"li\">One piece is a cap with one matching end, other end conditions decaying, and global stabilizer equal to the whole matching group \\(H\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The other piece and its unglued true-end limits are irreducible.</p><p class=\"li\">Frame the cap, leave the other piece unframed, and assume the matched problem, including flat parameters, metric parameters, and cuts, is isolated and transverse.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every match gives exactly one nearby solution for sufficiently long necks, and every converging solution is obtained this way. Regular and gauge-fixing inverses grow at most polynomially with length. Successive pendant-cap removal in a compatible tree has the same property. There is no ordering, central, or projective-twist multiplicity.</p>", "proof": "<p>The finite augmented operator has index\n\\[\ni_N^{\\rm fix}+i_V^{\\rm fix}+h^0+h^1+p+a-b=0.\n\\]\nAt a central \\(L\\)-flat, all three harmonic matching equations are retained. Framing alone does not remove the cap gauge-row cokernel.</p><p>For an extended homogeneous cap variation \\(a=\\tau\\,dt+\\beta+o(1)\\), integrate its gauge equation against a global parallel section. The one nondecaying boundary flux forces \\(\\tau=0\\); the cap stabilizer restricts onto the whole end stabilizer. Matching gives the same conclusion on the other half. The remaining fields and variables solve the isolated transverse half problem and vanish.</p><p>If no polynomial lower bound held, normalize approximate kernels in uniformly local norms with residual smaller than every fixed inverse power of length. Their half limits vanish. The zero-mode matching estimate\n\\[\n|z_T(T-R)-z_T(-T+R)|\n\\le CT\\|D_Tx_T\\|_{\\rm uloc}+Ce^{-\\delta R}\n\\]\nthen excludes norm escaping into the neck. Index zero gives invertibility. Polynomial losses are dominated by exponential pregluing errors, yielding existence and uniqueness. Cutting a converging solution at its common middle flat and using the injective half estimates gives exhaustion.</p><p>Remove pendant caps successively. Each cap absorbs the matching \\(H\\)-rotation. Nonzero framing degree changes charge sector; nonliftable projective twists are not allowed. Associative determinant-line gluing gives the orientations.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Theorem 2.1 and Corollary 2.20", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The rigid increasing maps \\( f_+:C(Y_0)\\to C(Y_2),\\qquad f_-:C(Y_{-2})\\to C(Y_0) \\) through \\(1\\) and \\(-1\\), respectively, are integral chain maps and mod-two units.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">A unit means an isomorphism on instanton homology over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(C_j\\) denote the knot trace from \\(S^3\\) to \\(Y_j\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix all end, collar, bundle, and tensor identifications before composing.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The rigid increasing maps\n\\[\nf_+:C(Y_0)\\to C(Y_2),\\qquad\nf_-:C(Y_{-2})\\to C(Y_0)\n\\]\nthrough \\(1\\) and \\(-1\\), respectively, are integral chain maps and mod-two units.</p><p>On \\(2\\to\\infty\\to0\\), with orthogonal classes \\(F_l^2=-2\\), \\(A^2=0\\), put \\(S_l=A-F_l\\). The paired actual-index-\\(-1\\) interval count for determinants\n\\[\nc(F_l,A)=(0,1),\\qquad c+\\operatorname{PD}(S_l)\n\\]\nis a mod-two chain map and unit \\(g_+\\). The mirror reversed construction gives the unit \\(g_-:C(Y_0;\\mathbb F_2)\\to C(Y_{-2};\\mathbb F_2)\\), with base evaluations \\((1,0)\\).</p><p>For an oriented diffeomorphism \\(\\varphi:Y_{-2}\\to Y_2\\),\n\\[\nT=f_-\\varphi^{-1}f_+:C(Y_0)\\to C(Y_0)\n\\]\nis an integral chain map, a mod-two unit, and a rational isomorphism. Its cobordism has\n\\[\nH_1(T;\\mathbb Z)=0,\\qquad \\chi(T)=4,\\qquad \\sigma(T)=-2,\n\\]\nand both increasing-handle halves are simply connected.</p>", "proof": "<p>Rigid chain identities and the composition defining \\(f_+\\) follow from the nonpositive-rank exclusions and irreducible gluing.</p><p>For a single increasing map \\(u_r\\), the two inverse pentagons have only the composition face and the outer continuation face: the \\(S^3\\)-face and both exceptional faces are empty. Their boundary relations over \\(\\mathbb F_2\\) are\n\\[\nu_rh_1\\simeq J_s,\\qquad h_2\\widetilde u_r\\simeq J_r,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\widetilde u_r\\) is tensor-equivalent to \\(u_r\\). Surjectivity and injectivity give equality of the finite homology dimensions and make \\(u_r\\) a unit.</p><p>The paired interval defining \\(g_+\\) is a chain map because its \\(S^3\\)-end is empty and its minimum \\(-2\\)-cap ends cancel. In the compressed pentagon the five terms are\n\\[\n0,\\quad f_+g_+,\\quad0,\\quad0,\\quad J_2,\n\\]\nso \\(f_+g_+\\simeq J_2\\), and \\(g_+\\) is a unit. Mirror reversal dualizes these constructions and gives \\(f_-,g_-\\).</p><p>The bridge is an integral chain map and a mod-two unit by composition. Its finite free mapping cone is mod-two acyclic, so its integral homology has no free summand and it is rationally acyclic. Normal generation gives simply connected handle halves and \\(H_1=0\\). Four two-handles give \\(\\chi=4\\); each half has one negative direction and no positive direction, giving \\(\\sigma=-2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemmas 2.5–2.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The quotient is the smooth local surgery model.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">A path of adjacent slopes has clockwise primitive vectors \\(w_0,\\ldots,w_m\\) with \\(\\det(w_i,w_{i+1})=-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Collapse the \\(w_i\\)-circle over side \\(i\\) of the polygon and the whole torus at corners.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The quotient is the smooth local surgery model. A primary arc gives \\(Y_{w_i}\\); an arc between sides \\(i,j\\) bounds the intervening linear plumbing, with adjacent intersections \\(+1\\) and weights determined by\n\\[\nw_{k-1}+w_{k+1}=d_kw_k,\\qquad S_k^2=-d_k.\n\\]\nEqual endpoint slopes give boundary \\(S^1\\times S^2\\), whose boundary sphere is the primitive radical. Replacing that plumbing by \\(S^1\\times D^3\\) gives the exterior identity cylinder.</p><p>Two-, three-, and four-step paths admit compatible interval, pentagon, and three-dimensional associahedral metric families. Simultaneous cuts are precisely noncrossing arc systems. Compressing the last two handles of \\(2,\\infty,0,1,2\\) gives a pentagon with cyclic faces\n\\[\nZ_1,Z_2,M_{02},M_{04},M_{14}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Adjacent slope circles form an integral basis, giving smooth bidisk corners. The basis change across a side has normal degree \\(-d_k\\), proving the weights and intersection signs. Exterior trivializations identify the pieces with the usual traces; deleting a \\(-1\\)-side is corner blowdown. Arc inverse images give the asserted cuts. Equal collapsed endpoint circles leave a transverse free circle and a primitive null sphere; filling by \\(S^1\\times D^3\\) joins the equal sides.</p><p>Choose disjoint collars for noncrossing arcs. Associahedral face coordinates prescribe independent collar lengths. Inductively choose product metrics on split pieces, with round rational links and product \\(S^1\\times S^2\\) links; extend over compact interiors using convexity of positive-definite tensors. Treating the last two handles as one fixed block removes its internal cut and yields the compressed face list. No twist coordinate is added.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.2", "orig": "Lemma 2.7", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The steps \\(r\\to r+1\\), \\(r=0,1\\), and their composite have \\(H_1=0\\), \\(b^+=0\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The steps \\(r\\to r+1\\), \\(r=0,1\\), and their composite have \\(H_1=0\\), \\(b^+=0\\). Allowed single-step determinants are fixed modulo twice an integral class and are tensor-equivalent.</p><p>The single-step inverse pentagons\n\\[\ns\\to\\infty\\to r\\to s,\\qquad\nr\\to s\\to\\infty\\to r,\\qquad s=r+1,\n\\]\nhave hard cap \\([-1,-1]\\), radical \\(P_1+P_2\\), and determinant evaluations \\((1,-1)\\), up to common sign. The compressed hard cap is \\([-2,-1,-2]\\), with radical \\(S_l+2T_1+R\\) and evaluations\n\\[\nc_0=(1,-1,1),\\qquad c_1=(-1,0,1).\n\\]\nAll full pentagons and interval subpieces have \\(b^+\\le1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Each meridional handle normally kills the incoming surgery group. Preceding a step by \\(C_r\\) gives a blowup of \\(C_{r+1}\\), with \\(G^2=r+1\\), \\(T^2=-1\\), incoming class \\(G-T\\). Its orthogonal complement has no positive direction: it is generated by \\(G-T\\) for \\(r=0\\), and \\(G-2T\\) for \\(r=1\\). The two-step calculation is the analogous twice-blown-up form. End restrictions determine determinant parity, and tensoring by a line identifies determinants differing by twice its class.</p><p>For the first inverse triple use\n\\[\nP_1=G-T-F_l,\\quad P_2=T,\\quad\nc(F_l,G,T)=(0,0,-1);\n\\]\nfor the second use \\(P_1=T\\), \\(P_2=G-F_l-T\\), \\(c=(1,1,1)\\). For the compressed path use squares \\((-2,2,-1,-1)\\) and\n\\[\nA=G-T_1-T_2,\\quad S_l=A-F_l,\\quad R=T_2-T_1,\n\\quad c_0=(0,0,-1,0).\n\\]\nDirect intersections give all displayed chains, radicals, evaluations, and positive-rank bounds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.3", "orig": "Lemmas 2.13–2.15", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Exceptional-sphere faces and the compressed \\(M_{14}\\)-face have index shift at least two relative to a flat-ball replacement.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Exceptional-sphere faces and the compressed \\(M_{14}\\)-face have index shift at least two relative to a flat-ball replacement. The odd-determinant \\(-2\\) cap has one trace-zero projective boundary class with \\((h^0,h^1)=(1,0)\\), and framed contribution\n\\[\n\\delta=8(\\mathcal E-\\tfrac18),\\qquad\n\\mathcal E\\in\\tfrac18+\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nAt minimum charge its framed match is one harmonic reducer; the two determinant terms agree and cancel modulo two.</p><p>A maximal group of \\(q\\) reducers with central rational contacts, \\(b_1=0\\), and two exterior matches contributes at least\n\\[\n3(1-b^+_{\\rm group}).\n\\]\nIn dimensions zero and one for the rigid, interval, and pentagon families, particles, unwanted central contacts, primary \\(S^3\\)-faces, exceptional faces, and \\(M_{14}\\) are absent. Only the paired minimum \\(M_{02}\\)-term can occur, away from corners.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a split difference \\(d\\), charge is \\(-d^2/4\\). An odd exceptional class has minimum charge \\(1/4\\), hence shift at least two. The \\(M_{14}\\) matrix and inverse are\n\\[\nQ=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&amp;1\\\\1&amp;-2\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad\nQ^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&amp;-1\\\\-1&amp;-1\\end{pmatrix};\n\\]\nboth prescribed determinant vectors have square \\(-1\\).</p><p>For the odd \\(-2\\) cap, \\(d^2=-1/2\\). Comparing \\(\\mathbb F_2\\) with \\(\\mathbb P(1,1,2)\\), the two normal twists have Euler characteristics \\(2,0\\) on both sides, so the framed normal index is zero. Tangential regularity follows from \\(H^1=b^+=0\\); the normal derivative is made invertible by Proposition 2. Tensoring the two cap sectors preserves the trace-zero boundary class and exterior data.</p><p>Summing the central index formula gives \\(-3q-3b^+_{\\rm group}\\); the \\(q+1\\) matches add \\(3(q+1)\\). The reference \\(-2\\) filling has zero shift and preserves this bound.</p><p>For intervals the relevant indices are \\(-1,0\\), and for pentagons \\(-2,-1\\). With no primary cut, a group not using positive rank has surplus three; one using the available positive rank needs two nonconstant true-end trajectories. With one cut it needs at least one such trajectory, while at most \\(p-1\\) parameters remain elsewhere. With two pentagon cuts no parameters remain and all lower bounds are nonnegative. These exceed the boundary indices. Positive cap shifts and higher sectors are likewise excluded. Particles have net dimension loss at least four per charge unit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.4", "orig": "Lemmas 2.16–2.17", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The replacement is an identity cylinder, possibly with a fixed end automorphism, and \\( i_{\\rm total}=i_{\\rm filled}+8\\mathcal E_N-3,\\qquad i_{\\rm filled}=i_V^{\\rm fix}+h^1,\\qquad \\mathcal E_N\\in\\tfrac14+\\mathbb Z. \\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">At the outer hard-cap face write the cap as \\(N\\), exterior as \\(V\\), and replace \\(N\\) by \\(D=S^1\\times D^3\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The replacement is an identity cylinder, possibly with a fixed end automorphism, and\n\\[\ni_{\\rm total}=i_{\\rm filled}+8\\mathcal E_N-3,\\qquad\ni_{\\rm filled}=i_V^{\\rm fix}+h^1,\\qquad\n\\mathcal E_N\\in\\tfrac14+\\mathbb Z.\n\\]\nAt total index \\(-1\\), only a regular irreducible continuation exterior with \\(i_{\\rm filled}=0\\), noncentral boundary flat, and \\(\\mathcal E_N=1/4\\) occurs.</p><p>For each cap metric, the minimum unordered split difference is \\(\\{c,-c\\}\\), with one harmonic ASD extension when the boundary flat varies. At a fixed noncentral flat, cap index and tangential unframed index are both \\(-2\\), and the complex normal index is zero. A transverse metric crossing gives one regular framed match.</p>", "proof": "<p>The determinant annihilates the primitive radical and extends over \\(D\\). Its flat family maps identically to the boundary flat slice and has fixed-limit index \\(-h^0\\). Since \\(\\chi+\\sigma\\) differs by two between either hard cap and \\(D\\), excision gives the index identity. Charge quantization gives \\(\\mathcal E_N=1/4+k\\), \\(k\\ge0\\), and hence \\(i_{\\rm filled}=-8k\\). Exterior regularity and the central exclusions force \\(k=0\\); fixing a central \\(L\\)-flat has codimension three. Stretching the filled cylinder and applying Proposition 2 identifies the exterior count with continuation.</p><p>For \\([-1,-1]\\), a radical-annihilating difference is \\((a,-a)\\), with square \\(-a^2\\), and \\(a\\) odd. For \\([-2,-1,-2]\\),\n\\[\nd_l+2d_m+d_r=0,\\qquad\n-\\frac{d^2}{4}=\\frac{d_l^2+d_r^2}{8}.\n\\]\nMinimum charge and parity give exactly the two stated determinant differences, up to sign.</p><p>Cylindrical Abelian Hodge theory gives the unique ASD curvature; exact ASD differences have zero square and vanish, and \\(H^1(N)=0\\) removes flat ambiguity. The tangential index has one stabilizer and one fixed-boundary flux obstruction. Framing and a transverse metric crossing remove them; the normal index-zero operator is made invertible. Ordering the lines and fixing the actual boundary eigenvalue creates no additional solution, and the cap torus absorbs matching rotations.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.5", "orig": "Lemmas 2.18–2.19", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The harmonic restriction path of the \\([-1,-1]\\) cap has odd mod-two degree at every generic noncentral boundary conjugacy class.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The harmonic restriction path of the \\([-1,-1]\\) cap has odd mod-two degree at every generic noncentral boundary conjugacy class. The sum of the two restriction degrees for \\([-2,-1,-2]\\) is odd. For generic exterior angles, the minimum matches are compact, avoid cap-interval endpoints, and glue to precisely one copy modulo two of the continuation exterior.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(\\beta\\) generate the free circle of \\(L\\), and choose a spanning chain \\(\\Delta\\subset N\\). With determinant boundary trivialization \\(\\tau\\), normalized ordered eigenholonomy is\n\\[\nu(d,c)=(-1)^{\\langle c,(\\Delta,\\tau)\\rangle}\n\\exp\\!\\left(\\pi i\\int_\\Delta d_{\\rm harm}\\right),\n\\]\nup to inversion. Boundary duality gives odd intersection of \\(\\Delta\\) with the primitive radical. Linear Hodge decay on rational links justifies curvature localization.</p><p>For \\([-1,-1]\\), the two localized differences are \\(-\\operatorname{PD}(P_1)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{PD}(P_2)\\); their endpoint values are opposite central values.</p><p>For the compressed cap, write \\(p=\\operatorname{PD}(S_l)\\) and\n\\[\nw=c_0+\\tfrac12p,\\qquad\nc_e=w+(e-\\tfrac12)p.\n\\]\nAt \\(S_l\\)-isolation, the curvature difference localizes to \\(p\\); its exponential sign cancels the change of half-determinant sign. The paths therefore have a common endpoint angle. At the other end their localized differences are\n\\[\n\\operatorname{PD}(T_1),\\qquad\n-\\operatorname{PD}(T_1)-\\operatorname{PD}(R).\n\\]\nChoose \\(\\Delta\\) outside this unimodular cap. Its radical pairing makes the determinant signs opposite, giving endpoints \\(0,\\pi\\). Endpoint parity therefore gives the odd degree.</p><p>Generic exterior angles avoid these finitely relevant endpoint values. Minimum charge excludes particles and positive collar levels. At \\(S_l\\)-isolation the reference-filled exterior has index \\(-2\\) with no parameter, so only the already computed harmonic endpoint could occur. Proposition 2 supplies exhaustive multiplicity-one gluing.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Theorem 3.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The actual-index-one interval-and-sweep counts define, with a local relative orientation convention, \\( B:C(Y_2;\\mathbb Z)\\longrightarrow C(Y_{-2};\\mathbb Z). \\) It is an integral chain map, its mod-two reduction …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let\n\\[\nW'=(-C_2)+C_{-2}:Y_2\\to Y_{-2}.\n\\]\nIts orthogonal trace classes satisfy \\(F_l^2=F_r^2=-2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The capping disks give an embedded sphere\n\\[\nS=F_l-F_r,\\qquad S^2=-4.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">The metric interval has primary endpoint \\(J=S^3\\) and lens endpoint separating \\(N'=\\nu S\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use determinants \\(c_e=e\\operatorname{PD}(S)\\), \\(e=0,1\\), identified off \\(N'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A degree-one sphere sweep \\(\\gamma_s\\), constant near its collapsed endpoints, defines\n\\[\nx(S):\\operatorname{Hol}_0(a,\\gamma_s)=-\\operatorname{id}.\n\\]\nNormalization uses the determinant root continued from the collapsed starting loop.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The actual-index-one interval-and-sweep counts define, with a local relative orientation convention,\n\\[\nB:C(Y_2;\\mathbb Z)\\longrightarrow C(Y_{-2};\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\nIt is an integral chain map, its mod-two reduction induces an isomorphism, and its integral chain-homotopy class is unchanged by the compatible data and representative homotopies specified below. The relative sign of the two determinant sectors is local, independent of the exterior, and preserved by composition.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 4.1–4.3 establish the integral count, its local signs, chain identity, and invariance. Lemma 4.4 identifies the four-step \\(Z_2\\)-term with a product of mod-two units. The associahedral boundary relation identifies that product with the remaining pentagon count. Lemmas 4.5–4.6 identify the latter with \\(B\\) modulo two. Thus \\(B\\) is a mod-two unit.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a fixed boundary flat \\(\\alpha\\) of the order-four lens cap …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a fixed boundary flat \\(\\alpha\\) of the order-four lens cap,\n\\[\n\\delta=i_{N'}^{\\rm fix}+h^0(\\alpha)=8\\mathcal E,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal E\\in\n\\begin{cases}\n\\mathbb Z,&amp;\\alpha\\text{ central},\\\\\n\\tfrac14+\\mathbb Z,&amp;\\alpha\\text{ trace zero}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nA flat cap misses \\(x(S)\\). The minimum tested cap has charge \\(1/4\\), trace-zero boundary, and framed real index two. Its regular framed tested solutions are harmonic reducer crossings with signed sum \\(\\pm1\\), each giving one match.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(x(S)=1\\), so \\(x^2=-1/4\\). The reference difference \\(d=2x\\) has charge \\(1/4\\). Fixed-boundary charge changes are integral. The central reference has framed index zero. For trace zero, compare \\(\\mathbb F_4\\) and \\(\\mathbb P(1,1,4)\\): the normal twists of degrees \\(2,-2\\) have Euler characteristics \\(3,-1\\) on the resolution and \\(3,0\\) on the weighted plane. Their reversed Dolbeault difference has complex index one; the tangential framed index is zero.</p><p>A minimum reducer has unordered difference \\(\\{\\pm2x\\}\\). Order it by \\(d(S)=2\\). Abelian Hodge theory and \\(H^1=0\\) give one ASD extension. Its normalized eigenline has degree one, so the sweep eigenvalue has winding \\(\\pm1\\). Make its \\(-1\\)-crossings transverse. Proposition 2 makes the complex normal index-one operator surjective while preserving the reducer, and makes the normal cut derivative an isomorphism. Together with the toral mark derivative,\n\\[\n2+1-3=0.\n\\]\nThe complex factor preserves orientation, leaving the winding sign. A framed irreducible would have a positive-dimensional residual torus orbit in a regular zero-dimensional locus and is absent. The reducer absorbs that torus; minimum charge leaves no further loss.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.2", "orig": "Proposition 3.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The two determinant lifts admit relative weights under which their minimum lens ends cancel over \\(\\mathbb Z\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The two determinant lifts admit relative weights under which their minimum lens ends cancel over \\(\\mathbb Z\\). The comparison preserves exterior relative degree, is independent of the exterior field, commutes with other gluings, and multiplies over disjoint caps. A coupled complex Dirac determinant contributes no additional relative sign.</p>", "proof": "<p>On the cap \\(\\operatorname{PD}(S)=-4x\\). Tensoring by a line of class \\(-2x\\) changes the determinant by \\(\\operatorname{PD}(S)\\) and gives\n\\[\n\\Delta c^2=-4,\\qquad \\Delta c_2=-1,\\qquad\n\\Delta(c_2-c^2/4)=0.\n\\]\nIts order-two boundary character sends \\(\\operatorname{diag}(i,-i)\\) to \\(\\operatorname{diag}(-i,i)\\). Fix a determinant-preserving parallel conjugation back to the common representative; its remaining ambiguity is connected.</p><p>Let\n\\[\nK_e=\\det D^{\\rm fr}_{N',e}\\otimes\\det\\mathbb R_s\n\\otimes(\\det\\mathfrak{su}(2))^*\n\\]\nand let \\(\\Gamma_e:\\lambda_V\\otimes K_e\\to\\lambda_e\\) be gluing. The full cap comparison \\(\\tau_{\\rm cap}\\) induces\n\\[\n\\Phi=\\Gamma_1(\\operatorname{id}\\otimes\\tau_{\\rm cap})\\Gamma_0^{-1}.\n\\]\nIf \\(\\Phi(o_0)=s\\,o_1\\), choose \\(\\varepsilon_1=-s\\varepsilon_0\\). This includes all Weyl, toral, complex-normal, framing, and target signs.</p><p>Tensor-related roots preserve normalized sweep holonomy; boundary conjugation preserves its central target. Connected reference spaces and orientable determinant systems extend the comparison with constant sign. Charge \\(1/4\\) fixes internal framing degree; degree-zero boundary gauges are nullhomotopic and extend over the cap. Associative gluing and even constrained cap parity give naturality and multiplicativity. The complex Dirac determinant has its canonical orientation and contributes sign \\(+1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.3", "orig": "Proposition 3.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "With Lemma 4.2’s signs, \\(B\\) is an integral chain map, and compatible homotopies give integral chain homotopies.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Representative homotopies are conjugation equivariant and relative to collapsed endpoints.</p><p class=\"li\">Their exact compact supports remain in the specified parts and persist away from support hits.</p><p class=\"li\">Equations do not depend on the incidence mark.</p><p class=\"li\">At the lens end, representatives remain cap-supported, tensor-paired, and disjoint from flat caps.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>With Lemma 4.2’s signs, \\(B\\) is an integral chain map, and compatible homotopies give integral chain homotopies. The representative homotopy need not be small.</p>", "proof": "<p>The rigid count has \\(i+1+1-3=0\\), hence \\(i=1\\); its boundary relation has \\(i=2\\). At \\(J\\), a central contact costs at least three even after discarding the cut. A true central end also requires a nonconstant trajectory, giving \\(4-1&gt;2\\). Bubbling is excluded by projecting to the equation problem after the index-eight loss.</p><p>At the lens endpoint a flat cap misses the cut and \\(\\delta\\ge2\\). The only possibility is\n\\[\ni=2,\\qquad \\delta=2,\\qquad i_{\\rm ext}=0,\n\\]\nwith one regular irreducible exterior and a minimum tested cap. Proposition 2 glues it, and Lemma 4.2 cancels the pair. The remaining ends are Floer breaks.</p><p>With one homotopy parameter, boundary index is one; the primary estimate is \\(3-1&gt;1\\), and the true-central estimate is \\(4-2&gt;1\\). The same lens cancellation applies. Pulling back the central degree-three class on \\(SU(2)\\) and integrating over the mark gives the degree-two transgression; endpoint-relative equivariant homotopy preserves it.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.4", "orig": "Lemmas 3.5–3.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The cobordism has \\(H_1=0\\), \\(b^+=1\\), and sphere chain \\([-2,0,-2]\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider the four-step path \\(2,\\infty,0,\\infty,-2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Its integral classes are \\(F_l,F_r,A,U\\), with \\(F_l^2=F_r^2=-2\\), \\(A^2=U^2=0\\), \\(AU=1\\), and other pairings zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(S_l=A-F_l\\), \\(S_r=A-F_r\\), and use\n\\[\nc=\\operatorname{PD}(U)+e\\operatorname{PD}(S_l)\n+e'\\operatorname{PD}(S_r).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The cobordism has \\(H_1=0\\), \\(b^+=1\\), and sphere chain \\([-2,0,-2]\\). Its \\(Z_2\\)-face is the product \\(g_-g_+\\), a mod-two unit. In the index-\\(-2\\) boundary relation, \\(M_{04},M_{03},M_{14},Z_1,Z_3\\) and their degenerations are empty; \\(M_{02},M_{24}\\) cancel under the corresponding flips. Only \\(Z_2\\), \\(M_{13}\\), and ordinary true-end breaks remain.</p>", "proof": "<p>The doubled zero trace has a zero-framed knot handle and linked meridian handle, giving the hyperbolic pair \\(A,U\\); normal generation gives \\(H_1=0\\). Restricting \\(\\operatorname{PD}(U)\\) to the two sides of \\(Z_2\\) gives the determinant data of \\(g_+,g_-\\).</p><p>For \\(M_{04}\\), the chain matrix is\n\\[\nQ=\\begin{pmatrix}-2&amp;1&amp;0\\\\1&amp;0&amp;1\\\\0&amp;1&amp;-2\\end{pmatrix},\n\\quad\nd^2=-\\frac{(d_l-d_r)^2}{4}+b(d_l+d_r)+b^2.\n\\]\nThe outer evaluations are odd, so charge lies in \\(\\frac14\\mathbb Z\\); zero is impossible by the odd-\\(w_2\\) sphere obstruction. Comparison with \\(N'\\) gives shift \\(8\\mathcal E-3\\ge-1\\), while the exterior has no parameters and nonnegative index. This exceeds \\(-2\\).</p><p>At \\(M_{03},M_{14}\\), \\(d^2=2b(d+b)\\) is divisible by four and charge is a positive integer, giving shift at least five. The small \\(-2\\) caps have only their reference minimum, paired modulo two. Central-group estimates, with at most one positive direction and the remaining parameters, exclude exceptional exteriors and corners. The two \\(S^3\\)-primary faces have no irreducible generators.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.5", "orig": "Lemmas 3.7–3.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Only \\(e=e'\\) permits a flat \\(L\\)-limit.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The remaining pentagon \\(P=M_{13}\\) separates \\(N_U=\\nu U\\cong S^2\\times D^2\\), with link \\(L=S^1\\times S^2\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Only \\(e=e'\\) permits a flat \\(L\\)-limit. After tensoring, the determinants are\n\\[\n\\operatorname{PD}(U)+e\\operatorname{PD}(F_l-F_r).\n\\]\nReplacing \\(N_U\\) by \\(D=S^1\\times D^3\\) gives \\(W'\\) with determinant \\(e\\operatorname{PD}(S)\\). In the exterior root normalization the flat cap selects holonomy \\(-\\operatorname{id}\\).</p><p>At total index \\(-2\\), \\(P\\) consists of isolated regular selected-center matches with flat \\(N_U\\), and its map is a mod-two unit. At a sufficiently long finite replacement neck it equals the index-one count on \\(W'\\) over \\(P\\) with\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Hol}_0(a,\\beta)=-\\operatorname{id},\n\\qquad \\beta=S^1\\times\\{0\\}\\subset D.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The determinant evaluates on \\(U\\) as \\(e+e'\\); flatness on \\(L\\) requires even evaluation. Tensoring the \\(e=e'=1\\) sector by \\(-\\operatorname{PD}(S_l)\\) gives the displayed determinant.</p><p>Roots exist separately on cap and exterior. Their mismatch is the nontrivial sign line in \\(H^1(L;\\mathbb Z/2)\\): otherwise they would glue to a global root, contradicting \\(c(A)=1\\). Thus the cap’s \\(+1\\)-holonomy becomes \\(-1\\) in the exterior normalization.</p><p>Index excision gives\n\\[\ni_{\\rm filled}=i_V^{\\rm fix}+h^1\n=i_{\\rm total}+3-8\\mathcal E_{N_U}\\le1.\n\\]\nPositive integer cap or neck charge is excluded. Fixing the central flat imposes three equations, so\n\\[\ni_{\\rm filled}+p-3\\le1+2-3=0,\n\\]\nwith equality only for flat cap and two pentagon parameters. Its three flux obstructions are removed by the flat parameters. Proposition 2 gives coefficient-one gluing, and Lemma 4.4 identifies the map with \\(g_-g_+\\).</p><p>After replacement, the flat \\(D\\)-family evaluates identically on generator holonomy. The loop test fixes its selected flat. On boundary edges, nonflat caps cost too much index; zero-charge simply connected caps contain \\(\\beta\\) and have normalized holonomy \\(+1\\). Harmonic-neck estimates also exclude escaping marks down the replacement neck.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 4.6", "orig": "Lemma 3.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The pentagon loop count in Lemma 4.5 agrees modulo two with the interval map \\(B\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The pentagon loop count in Lemma 4.5 agrees modulo two with the interval map \\(B\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>After \\(U\\)-replacement, the merged \\(\\infty\\)-side lifts to a sphere. The circle \\(\\beta\\) divides it into disks \\(\\Delta_l,\\Delta_r\\), whose union represents \\(\\pm(F_l-F_r)=\\pm S\\). Both disks lie in the outer lens cap; each lies in the appropriate incompatible one-corner ball cap.</p><p>Choose an interval \\(I\\subset P\\) separating the two ball edges. Contract \\(\\beta\\) through \\(\\Delta_l\\) on one region and through \\(\\Delta_r\\) on the other. These are one-dimensional constrained families:\n\\[\ni'+2+1-3=1.\n\\]\nPrimary and true-central equation projections are empty independently of the contraction mark. Positive losses and particles remain excluded. Zero-charge caps miss the test because the contraction lies in their simply connected interiors; collapsed loops also miss it. The contractions may use uniformly bounded free-circle loops in neck slices, and Proposition 2 gives uniform avoidance even for escaping marks.</p><p>The two mod-two boundary formulas leave the concatenated disk contraction on \\(I\\):\n\\[\ni'+1+1-3=0.\n\\]\nThis is the sphere-sweep interval count. Insert tensor-paired lens data near the empty endpoint and use Lemma 4.3 to compare with the chosen \\(B\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Lemma 4.2 and Proposition 4.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a symplectic closure \\(X_0\\) of \\(Y_0\\), with simply connected halves, symplectic \\(A\\), and a tight genus-two surface in each half.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The capped minimal Seifert surface \\(A\\subset Y_0\\) has genus two.</p><p class=\"li\">A tight surface \\(B\\) means an embedded oriented surface with \\(B^2=2g(B)-2&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize \\(\\mu(\\xi)=-\\tfrac14p_1(\\operatorname{ad}E)/\\xi\\), and write \\(x_0\\) for the point class.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a symplectic closure \\(X_0\\) of \\(Y_0\\), with simply connected halves, symplectic \\(A\\), and a tight genus-two surface in each half. It may satisfy\n\\[\nq(X_0)=\\frac{\\chi+\\sigma}{4}\\ge3,\\qquad\ns(X_0)=2\\chi+3\\sigma\\ge q(X_0)-2.\n\\]\nThe class \\(A\\) is primitive. For an integral \\(w\\) with \\(wA=1\\), there are \\(m,a\\ge0\\) and \\(h\\in H_2(X_0;\\mathbb Z)\\) such that\n\\[\nhA=1,\\qquad D_{X_0}^{w}(x_0^mh^a)\\ne0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the pencil of Lemma 5.3 and the elliptic model of Lemma 5.4 at the same genus. Removing two regular disks from the model gives an annular map \\(M\\) commuting with \\(S_\\mu,P_\\mu\\). Polynomial generalized spectral projectors and the nonzero extreme exponential coefficient give an extreme line \\(V_*\\) with\n\\[\nv_g\\Pi_*M\\Pi_*u_g\\ne0.\n\\]\nLemma 5.2 makes \\(V_*\\) one-dimensional, so both projected disk states and the scalar action of \\(M\\) are nonzero. The paper’s alternative growth argument gives the same conclusion independently of monomial orientation signs.</p><p>For each twist \\(a_i\\) of the pencil word, conjugate a model word to begin with \\(a_i\\). Extract the selected twists in order using\n\\[\nRa=a(a^{-1}Ra).\n\\]\nThe product and desired prefix are pointed identity words, so the remaining suffix is also one. Their maps preserve \\(V_*\\), and the total scalar is nonzero; hence the pencil prefix acts nontrivially. Track the actual section and its determinant through Hurwitz moves. In the boundary-fixed mapping class group the central boundary-twist exponent records the section’s normal Euler number and is preserved for the extracted prefix. No extra loop-of-identifications invariant appears because \\(\\pi_1(\\operatorname{Diff}_0(\\Sigma_g,p))=0\\).</p><p>Cap with the nonzero projected disk states. Since projectors are polynomials, some fiber-and-point monomial gives a nonzero Donaldson invariant on the blowup, by Lemma 5.5. The tight-surface simple-type theorem, the basic-class structure theorem, and the blowup formula transfer nonvanishing to the prescribed determinant \\(w\\) on \\(X_0\\): changing determinant changes nonzero basic coefficients only by signs.</p><p>Some homogeneous polynomial \\(h\\mapsto D_{X_0}^w(x_0^mh^a)\\) is therefore nonzero. The integral affine lattice \\(hA=1\\) is nonempty and Zariski dense in that hyperplane. A nonzero homogeneous polynomial cannot vanish on the entire hyperplane, since rescaling would make it vanish on \\(hA\\ne0\\). Choose the required integral \\(h\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The enhanced closure with the topology, tight surfaces, geography, and primitive \\(A\\) asserted in Proposition 5 exists.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The enhanced closure with the topology, tight surfaces, geography, and primitive \\(A\\) asserted in Proposition 5 exists.</p>", "proof": "<p>Gabai’s foliation and contact approximation embed a symplectic neighborhood of \\(Y_0\\) with \\(A\\) symplectic. Choose connected-binding open books of page genus\n\\[\nG=(d-1)(d-2)/2,\\qquad d\\ge6,\n\\]\nand attach symplectic binding handles. Factor inverse monodromy into positive nonseparating twists, then append plane-pencil positive identity blocks. One such block normally kills the fiber group: a plane-pencil complement is simply connected because its fiber meridian bounds a punctured section. The new boundary surjects onto the binding-handle cobordism group, so the capped half is simply connected.</p><p>Since \\(H^3(Z,\\partial Z;\\mathbb R)=0\\), the prescribed boundary two-form extends. Patch fiber-positive representatives relative to the boundary and add a large pullback of a disk area form; its boundary tangential restriction is zero.</p><p>Adjacent identity blocks provide a matching \\(-2\\)-sphere and a rim torus meeting once. Resolve the sphere’s intersections with two parallel rim tori:\n\\[\n\\chi(B)=2-4=-2,\\qquad B^2=-2+4=2.\n\\]\nAdditional blocks give\n\\[\n\\Delta q=G,\\qquad \\Delta s=1-d^2+8G,\n\\]\nso sufficiently many achieve the geography.</p><p>Adjunction gives \\(K_\\omega A=2\\). If \\(A=ka\\), then \\(k=2\\), \\(a^2=0\\), \\(K_\\omega a=1\\), contradicting characteristic parity. Thus \\(A\\) is primitive, and unimodularity supplies \\(wA=1\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every joint eigenvalue \\((\\lambda,\\nu)\\) has \\(|\\lambda|\\le2g-2\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(V_g\\) be the full determinant-one instanton homology over \\(\\mathbb C\\) of \\(\\Sigma_g\\times S^1\\), determinant dual to \\(\\{p\\}\\times S^1\\), \\(g\\ge2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(S_\\mu=\\mu(\\Sigma_g)\\), \\(P_\\mu=\\mu(x_0)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every joint eigenvalue \\((\\lambda,\\nu)\\) has \\(|\\lambda|\\le2g-2\\). Equality occurs exactly at\n\\[\n(\\lambda,\\nu)=\\bigl(i^r(2g-2),(-1)^r2\\bigr),\n\\qquad r=0,1,2,3,\n\\]\nand each corresponding simultaneous generalized eigenspace is one-dimensional.</p>", "proof": "<p>In Muñoz’s involution quotient use \\(\\alpha=2S_\\mu\\), \\(\\beta=-4P_\\mu\\), \\(\\gamma\\), and\n\\[\nR_g^1=\\alpha R_{g-1}^1+(g-1)^2R_{g-1}^2,\n\\quad\nR_g^2=(\\beta+(-1)^g8)R_{g-1}^1+\\frac{2(g-1)}gR_{g-1}^3,\n\\quad\nR_g^3=\\gamma R_{g-1}^1.\n\\]\nThe extreme points are \\((\\pm4(g-1)i^g,(-1)^{g-1}8,0)\\). With \\(\\zeta_r=R_r^1\\),\n\\[\n\\zeta_{r+1}=\\alpha\\zeta_r+r^2(\\beta+(-1)^r8)\\zeta_{r-1}\n+2r(r-1)\\gamma\\zeta_{r-2}.\n\\]\nAt the extreme \\(\\beta\\)-value and \\(\\gamma=0\\), its factors are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{ll}\n\\beta=-8:&amp;\nf_{2m}=\\prod_{j=1}^m(\\alpha^2-16(2j-1)^2),\\quad f_{2m+1}=\\alpha f_{2m},\\\\\n\\beta=8:&amp;\nf_{2m}=\\alpha^2\\prod_{j=1}^{m-1}(\\alpha^2+64j^2),\\quad\nf_{2m+1}=\\alpha\\prod_{j=1}^{m}(\\alpha^2+64j^2).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThus \\(R_{g-1}^1\\) is a local unit and the extreme root of \\(R_g^1\\) is simple. The relations successively force \\(\\gamma=0\\), \\(\\beta=\\beta_0\\), \\(\\alpha=\\alpha_0\\); the local algebra is \\(\\mathbb C\\). Primitive exterior-power summands correspond to smaller genus and contribute no extreme eigenvalue.</p><p>The grading character \\(U\\) on the full determinant-one group satisfies\n\\[\nS_\\mu U=iUS_\\mu,\\qquad P_\\mu U=-UP_\\mu,\\qquad U\\tau=-\\tau U.\n\\]\nIt exchanges the involution sectors and supplies the other two extreme lines.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.3", "orig": "Lemma 4.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After blowups disjoint from \\(A\\), the tight surfaces, and \\(Y_0\\), \\(X_0\\) admits a genus-\\(g\\) Lefschetz fibration with section, \\(g\\ge2\\), all vanishing cycles nonseparating.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After blowups disjoint from \\(A\\), the tight surfaces, and \\(Y_0\\), \\(X_0\\) admits a genus-\\(g\\) Lefschetz fibration with section, \\(g\\ge2\\), all vanishing cycles nonseparating. Its positive word is the identity in the mapping class group with one marked point.</p>", "proof": "<p>Take a sufficiently high-degree Donaldson pencil. For its fiber \\(F_0\\), basepoint number \\(b=F_0^2\\), and genus \\(g_0\\), Taubes’s canonical inequality and adjunction give \\(2g_0-2\\ge b\\). Apply full degree doubling. An old separating cycle remains separating only if one side contains no used basepoint. Every component \\(C\\) of a reducible pencil fiber has \\(F_0C=k\\int_C\\omega&gt;0\\), and distinct fibers meet it only at basepoints; therefore both sides contain used basepoints. All doubled vanishing cycles are nonseparating. Blow up the doubled basepoints, chosen away from the specified subsets. An exceptional section gives the marked-point identity word.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.4", "orig": "Lemma 4.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For every \\(g\\ge2\\), a genus-\\(g\\) Lefschetz fibration with section exists on a manifold deformation equivalent to \\(E(g+1)\\), with only nonseparating vanishing cycles, whose section-determinant Donaldson series has a …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every \\(g\\ge2\\), a genus-\\(g\\) Lefschetz fibration with section exists on a manifold deformation equivalent to \\(E(g+1)\\), with only nonseparating vanishing cycles, whose section-determinant Donaldson series has a nonzero coefficient at \\(e^{(2g-2)t}\\) on the fiber line.</p>", "proof": "<p>Set \\(n=g+1\\). Take the double cover of \\(\\mathbb P_x^1\\times\\mathbb P_y^1\\) branched over a smooth divisor of bidegree \\((4,2n)\\), imposing \\(b(x,y_0)=s(x)^2\\) for a quadratic \\(s\\) with simple zeros. The curves \\(z=\\pm s(x)\\) are sections. Require \\(\\partial b/\\partial y\\ne0\\) there. Variations divisible by \\(y-y_0\\) supply the jets excluding singular branch points, triple vertical roots, and two double roots in one fiber. Each singular fiber has one nonseparating node.</p><p>A generic \\((2,2)\\)-pencil realizes the \\((4,2)\\) double cover as the cubic-pencil \\(E(1)\\): after eight blowups, \\(|H_x+H_z-E_1|\\) contracts nine disjoint exceptional curves and sends the fiber to a cubic through nine basepoints. Cyclic degree-\\(n\\) base change, branched over two regular values, gives the untwisted fiber sum \\(E(n)\\). Smooth \\((4,2n)\\) branch divisors transverse to a fixed ruling fiber form a connected open set, giving a deformation and a diffeomorphism preserving the elliptic fiber class \\(F\\).</p><p>For the genus-\\(g\\) fiber \\(G_f\\), \\(FG_f=2\\), \\(G_f^2=0\\). The elliptic Donaldson series has nonzero extreme basic classes \\(\\pm(n-2)F\\), with factor \\(\\sinh(F)^{n-2}\\) in the relevant convention. Hence the extreme exponents on \\(tG_f\\) are \\(\\pm(2g-2)t\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.5", "orig": "Lemma 4.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Their closed exact-test counts are the fixed-determinant Donaldson invariants with each insertion replaced by \\(4\\mu\\), without an additional determinant-lift sum.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Consider the simply connected closed gluings used in Proposition 5 and, subsequently, the closures obtained by inserting copies of the bridge \\(T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use actual determinant-preserving transition functions and the exact tests of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">The closed manifolds retain a tight surface and \\(b^+&gt;1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Their closed exact-test counts are the fixed-determinant Donaldson invariants with each insertion replaced by \\(4\\mu\\), without an additional determinant-lift sum.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a long admissible collar, a reducible has odd line-difference evaluation on the odd surface slice. Pairing with a fixed dual form gives a positive slice-curvature lower bound; integrating along the collar contradicts bounded charge. Gradient Chern–Simons errors telescope, so turn off the Floer perturbation while the collar remains long. Then use a generic closed metric path; \\(b^+&gt;1\\) avoids the finitely relevant reducible walls. Proposition 2’s particle budget applies.</p><p>Localize tests near their surface and point representatives. Irreducible ASD fields on these simply connected manifolds are locally irreducible by unique continuation. Omitting one surface test leaves a compact two-dimensional partially cut space: a particle releases at most degree four per charge, and projection to retained local equations gives dimension \\(2-8\\ell+4\\ell&lt;0\\). On that space the moving-point count is exactly the slant product of \\(c_2(\\operatorname{ad}_{\\mathbb C}E)\\), hence \\(4\\mu\\).</p><p>Nonliftable projective seam automorphisms are not permitted. Components of the determinant-one gauge group are classified by \\(H^3(Y;\\mathbb Z)\\); nonzero degree changes \\(c_2\\) and index by eight, and actual index fixes the total degree. Full boundary orbits are retained, not quotiented by nonliftable twists. Restrictions of every closed connection are therefore included, and conversely full-state matches with fixed total \\(c_1,c_2\\) glue the prescribed bundle. Ordinary gluing has multiplicity one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 5.1; Lemmas 5.2–5.3; Corollary 5.4; Lemma 8.2", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The resulting cell lattices and characteristic lifts have the formulas below.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Take two fixed caps with rational boundary \\(Y_2\\), \\(H_1=0\\), determinant trivialized at the boundary, and fixed probes of total real degree \\(z\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Insert \\(n\\) copies of \\(W'\\). Return from \\(Y_{-2}\\) to \\(Y_2\\) by \\(\\varphi\\), except at \\(m\\) selected locations where the increasing path\n\\[\nH=f_+f_-:Y_{-2}\\to Y_2\n\\]\nis used.</p><p class=\"li\">Selected positive bridges are at least four intervals apart; long initial and terminal pads have none.</p><p class=\"li\">A positive bridge and its adjacent intervals form a macro. Let \\(J_i\\) be the primary cut and \\(S_i=F_{l,i}-F_{r,i}\\) the \\(-4\\)-sphere in interval \\(i\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Cut at all \\(J_i\\) and fill spherical boundaries by balls.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The resulting cell lattices and characteristic lifts have the formulas below. With \\(l=\\Lambda-c\\), define\n\\[\n\\kappa=c_2(E)-c^2/4,\\qquad\n\\Theta=(\\Lambda^2-\\sigma)/4.\n\\]\nThe closed indices are\n\\[\nD_I=8\\kappa-3(1+b^+),\\qquad\nn_D=\\Theta-\\kappa,\\qquad D_{\\rm sp}=D_I+2n_D.\n\\]\nThe ordinary zero-dimensional condition is \\(D_I=n+z\\). For fixed outside data,\n\\[\nb^+=m+b_0,\\qquad \\Theta=m+\\Theta_0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nn_D=\\frac{5m-n}{8}+\\Theta_0-\\frac{z+3+3b_0}{8}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The cell and lift formulas follow from Lemmas 6.1–6.2, and the index formulas from Lemma 6.3. The ordinary parameter-and-cut dimension is\n\\[\nD_I+n-2n-z,\n\\]\nso zero dimension means \\(D_I=n+z\\). Substitute \\(b^+=m+b_0\\), \\(\\Theta=m+\\Theta_0\\) into \\(n_D=\\Theta-\\kappa\\). For the repeated pattern \\(n=4m+d\\), the coupled index grows as \\(m/8\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Proposition 5.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "All filled pieces have \\(H_1=0\\) and unimodular integral forms.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>All filled pieces have \\(H_1=0\\) and unimodular integral forms. A negative cell has basis\n\\[\na=\\frac{F_r+F_l'}2,\\qquad b=\\frac{F_r-F_l'}2,\n\\qquad a^2=b^2=-1,\\quad ab=0.\n\\]\nA positive cell has basis \\(G,U,T_1,\\ldots,T_4\\), with\n\\[\nQ_P=\\begin{pmatrix}2&amp;1\\\\1&amp;0\\end{pmatrix}\\oplus(-I_4),\n\\quad F_r=G-\\sum T_b,\\quad F_l'=G-2U,\\quad\nF_0=G-T_3-T_4.\n\\]\nThe macro contains the plumbing\n\\[\nX_1=-S_j,\\quad U,\\quad X_2=S_{j+1},\n\\qquad (-4,0,-4),\n\\]\nwith \\(UX_1=UX_2=1\\), \\(X_1X_2=0\\). For evaluations \\(v=(x,u;t_1,\\ldots,t_4)\\),\n\\[\nv_P^2=2xu-2u^2-\\sum t_b^2,\\qquad\nv_N^2=-\\frac{(vF_r)^2+(vF_l')^2}{2}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Mayer–Vietoris across rational seams preserves \\(H_1=0\\); filling spherical boundaries does not change it. Poincaré duality gives unimodularity. The negative trace lattice \\(\\operatorname{diag}(-2,-2)\\) has index two. Its only integral discriminant extension adjoins \\((F_r+F_l')/2\\), yielding the stated basis independently of the arbitrary \\(\\varphi\\).</p><p>For a positive cell, the consecutive polygon rays give the chain \\([-1,-2,-2,-2,-1]\\). Smoothing the sum gives a square-zero sphere \\(U\\), meeting the adjacent test spheres once. Blow down the four increasing-handle corners, leaving the doubled \\(2\\)-trace with \\(G^2=2\\), \\(GU=1\\), \\(U^2=0\\); restore total exceptional transforms \\(T_b\\). Proper transforms give the formulas for \\(F_r,F_l',F_0\\). The local corner charts give the positive plumbing intersections. Inverting the rank-two matrix gives the square formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.2", "orig": "Lemmas 5.2–5.3 and Corollary 5.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On negative cells choose \\(c_0=0\\), \\((\\Lambda_0F_r,\\Lambda_0F_l')=(-2,0)\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On negative cells choose \\(c_0=0\\), \\((\\Lambda_0F_r,\\Lambda_0F_l')=(-2,0)\\). On positive cells choose\n\\[\nc_0=(2,1;1,0,1,0),\\qquad\n\\Lambda_0=(-2,-1;0,1,0,-1).\n\\]\nExtend the fixed cap determinants by zero on bordering traces; choose \\(l=\\Lambda_0-c_0\\) characteristic, with the stated outside trace values and arbitrary odd values on the separate extra exceptional classes. Then\n\\[\nc_e=c_0+\\sum e_i\\operatorname{PD}(S_i),\\qquad\n\\Lambda_e=\\Lambda_0+\\sum e_i\\operatorname{PD}(S_i)\n\\]\nare integral, \\(l\\) is unchanged, and\n\\[\nc_0S_i=0,\\quad\\Lambda_0S_i=2,\\quad\nc_e^2=c_0^2-4\\sum e_i,\\quad \\Lambda_e^2=\\Lambda_0^2.\n\\]\nAn interior segment of \\(L\\) cells with \\(m_I\\) positive cells and endpoint bits \\(e_a,e_b\\) has\n\\[\nb_I^+=m_I,\\qquad\n\\Theta_I=m_I+\\frac{e_a-e_b}{2},\\qquad p_I=L-1.\n\\]\nIf \\(m_I\\ge1\\), then \\(L\\ge4m_I-3\\), with endpoint offsets added to this bound.</p>", "proof": "<p>On a negative cell \\(l\\) evaluates as \\((-1,-1)\\) on the \\(-I_2\\) basis. On a positive cell it evaluates as \\((-4,-2;-1,1,-1,-1)\\), exactly the characteristic parity. A square-\\(-2\\) class is primitive; unimodularity supplies a dual class, allowing even adjustment to the prescribed outside evaluation. Glue across \\(S^3\\)-seams.</p><p>Direct evaluation gives even determinant restrictions on rational seams and odd value on \\(F_0\\). Disjoint sphere supports give the square formulas. On a cell between \\(J_j,J_{j+1}\\), the bit change is\n\\[\n-e_j\\operatorname{PD}(F_r)+e_{j+1}\\operatorname{PD}(F_l'),\n\\]\nchanging \\(\\Lambda^2\\) by \\(2(e_j-e_{j+1})\\). The base \\((\\Lambda^2-\\sigma)/4\\) is zero on negative cells and one on positive cells. Endpoint differences telescope, including the outside contributions. Spacing four gives \\(L\\ge4m_I-3\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.3", "orig": "Lemma 8.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a closed filled piece with \\(b_1=0\\), the indices are those in Proposition 6.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a closed filled piece with \\(b_1=0\\), the indices are those in Proposition 6. At a split field whose active spinor line has determinant class \\(K\\), the pure-line tangential index is\n\\[\nd_s=\\frac{K^2-(2\\chi+3\\sigma)}4.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The ASD symbol gives \\(8\\kappa-3(1+b^+)\\). The degree-four term of \\(\\operatorname{ch}(E)e^{l/2}\\widehat A\\) is\n\\[\n\\frac{c^2}{2}-c_2(E)+\\frac{cl}{2}+\\frac{l^2}{4}-\\frac{\\sigma}{4}\n=\\frac{(l+c)^2-\\sigma}{4}-\\kappa.\n\\]\nThis gives \\(n_D=\\Theta-\\kappa\\). The complex Dirac summand contributes twice its index to the real deformation index. For one line, add twice \\((K^2-\\sigma)/8\\) to \\(-(1-b_1+b^+)\\), obtaining \\(d_s\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Theorem 6.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There is a smooth interval-cube family, for sufficiently small width \\(\\epsilon>0\\), sufficiently small regularizer \\(d>0\\), and arbitrarily long finite auxiliary rational collars, with no additional parameter faces …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix a finite stack, its lifts, bit choices, exceptional evaluations, and charge ranges as in Proposition 6.</p><p class=\"li\">Only \\(J_i\\cong S^3\\) and the \\(-4\\)-lens boundaries are genuine infinite cuts. Surgery seams and all auxiliary isolation collars remain finite.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a smooth interval-cube family, for sufficiently small width \\(\\epsilon&gt;0\\), sufficiently small regularizer \\(d&gt;0\\), and arbitrarily long finite auxiliary rational collars, with no additional parameter faces, having:</p><p class=\"li\">independent product genuine collars, round \\(J_i\\), parameter locality on split components, and positive-scalar separated lens caps with fixed link scale;</p><p class=\"li\">positive-width retained toric main metrics conformal to complete Kähler metrics with positive ray\n\\[\nH=U+aX_1+bX_2,\\qquad\n0\\le a,b\\le\\tfrac14,\\quad a+b&gt;0;\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">nonnegative scalar curvature, strict positivity on an open set, and \\(b^+=1\\) on those main pieces;</p><p class=\"li\">a small-width tube of length comparable to \\(\\epsilon^{-1}\\), fiber \\(U\\), and bulk model\n\\[\ng_{\\rm long}=g_{S^2(1/\\sqrt2)}+\\tfrac12d\\xi^2+2d\\theta_x^2;\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">the uniform geometric and comparison-form estimates established below.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 7.1–7.3 construct the toric, satellite, and positive cap models. Lemma 7.4 assembles them with precisely the genuine faces and the required parameter locality, and covers the small-width corner. Intervals outside macros use ordinary metric paths with the same true endpoints. Different macros and auxiliary seams are disjoint, so their constructions form products. Lemma 7.5 supplies the cohomology and uniform error assertions. Only spatial derivative bounds are required as lengths diverge.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.1", "orig": "Lemmas 6.2–6.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The neighborhoods \\( C_2=\\nu(X_1\\cup U\\cup X_2),\\quad C_{1,i}=\\nu(U\\cup X_{3-i}),\\quad C_0=\\nu U,\\quad N_i=\\nu X_i \\) can be placed with \\(U\\cap(J_1\\cup J_2)=\\varnothing\\), \\(C_{1,i}\\cap J_i=\\varnothing\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The neighborhoods\n\\[\nC_2=\\nu(X_1\\cup U\\cup X_2),\\quad\nC_{1,i}=\\nu(U\\cup X_{3-i}),\\quad C_0=\\nu U,\\quad N_i=\\nu X_i\n\\]\ncan be placed with \\(U\\cap(J_1\\cup J_2)=\\varnothing\\), \\(C_{1,i}\\cap J_i=\\varnothing\\), \\(N_{3-i}\\subset C_{1,i}\\), and \\(C_0\\subset C_{1,1}\\cap C_{1,2}\\). Required interfaces are disjoint or nested; incompatible low interfaces are never simultaneous.</p><p>The punctured polygon\n\\[\n4|y|\\le x\\le1,\\qquad -a\\le y\\le b\n\\]\nmodels \\(C_2\\). With \\(w=a+b\\), \\(\\xi=x/w\\), \\(y'=y/w\\), \\(L=w^{-1}\\), \\(r_1=a/w\\), \\(r_2=b/w\\),\n\\[\ng=\\tfrac12M_{ij}d\\mu_i d\\mu_j+2(M^{-1})_{ij}d\\theta_i d\\theta_j,\n\\quad\nM=C+\\sum_\\nu\\frac{n_\\nu n_\\nu^t}{l_\\nu},\n\\quad C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\0&amp;0\\end{pmatrix},\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\nn_\\nu=(1,4),(0,1),(-1,0),(0,-1),(1,-4),\n\\]\n\\[\nl_\\nu=\\xi+4y',\\ r_1+y',\\ L-\\xi,\\ r_2-y',\\ \\xi-4y'.\n\\]\nIts periods satisfy\n\\[\nHX_1=1-4a,\\quad HU=a+b,\\quad HX_2=1-4b,\\quad\nH^2=2(a+b)-4(a^2+b^2)&gt;0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The cocore double in the positive cell is \\(U\\); it avoids the four corner blowups and both middle cuts. Axis-coordinate plumbing gives the embedded \\((-4,0,-4)\\) configuration and the stated neighborhood placements.</p><p>Successive polygon normal pairs have determinant one except the removed order-eight corner. The relations\n\\[\nn_p+n_o=4n_1,\\quad n_1+n_2=0,\\quad n_o+n_q=4n_2\n\\]\ngive the three divisor weights. The potential\n\\[\n\\tfrac14\\xi^2+\\tfrac12\\sum_\\nu l_\\nu\\log l_\\nu\n\\]\nhas Hessian \\(M/2\\) and the standard smooth primitive-facet extension. Affine edge lengths give the periods. Inverting the plumbing matrix identifies their class with \\(H\\).</p><p>At a zero coefficient retain the corresponding subchain and its redundant limiting affine term. At coefficient \\(1/4\\), orthogonality to \\(X_i\\) puts \\(H\\) on the complement of \\(N_i\\). Rational links give nondegenerate orthogonal cohomology splittings; removing negative directions leaves positive rank one.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.2", "orig": "Lemmas 6.4–6.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(B=M^{-1}\\), put \\( A_\\nu=B^{1/2}n_\\nu/\\sqrt{l_\\nu},\\quad P_{\\nu\\mu}=A_\\nu^tA_\\mu,\\quad z_\\nu=l_\\nu^{-1/2},\\quad q_\\nu=z_\\nu P_{\\nu\\nu}. \\) Then \\(0\\le P\\le I\\) and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(B=M^{-1}\\), put\n\\[\nA_\\nu=B^{1/2}n_\\nu/\\sqrt{l_\\nu},\\quad\nP_{\\nu\\mu}=A_\\nu^tA_\\mu,\\quad\nz_\\nu=l_\\nu^{-1/2},\\quad q_\\nu=z_\\nu P_{\\nu\\nu}.\n\\]\nThen \\(0\\le P\\le I\\) and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Scal}(g)=2\\mathcal S,\\qquad\n\\mathcal S=q^t(I-P)q+\nz^t\\bigl((I-P)\\circ(P\\circ P)\\bigr)z\\ge0.\n\\]\nFor every nonzero relation \\(\\sum\\gamma_\\nu n_\\nu=0\\),\n\\[\nq^t(I-P)q\\ge\n\\frac{(\\sum\\gamma_\\nu P_{\\nu\\nu})^2}{\\sum\\gamma_\\nu^2l_\\nu},\n\\]\n\\[\nz^t((I-P)\\circ(P\\circ P))z\\ge\n\\frac{\\|\\sum\\gamma_\\nu A_\\nu A_\\nu^t\\|_{\\rm HS}^2}\n{\\sum\\gamma_\\nu^2l_\\nu}.\n\\]\nMain and three-direction satellite metrics have the stated strict compact positivity. Homogeneous puncture metrics are cones whose smooth links have scalar curvature at least six; a collapsed-\\(X_i\\) link is joined through positive-scalar metrics to the round lens metric.</p>", "proof": "<p>Writing \\(A\\) for the column matrix gives\n\\[\nAA^t=I-B^{1/2}CB^{1/2}\\le I.\n\\]\nDifferentiating \\(BM=I\\), then contracting second derivatives, gives\n\\[\n-\\partial_i\\partial_jB_{ij}\n=2\\sum_\\nu\\frac{G_{\\nu\\nu}^2}{l_\\nu^3}\n-\\sum_{\\nu,\\mu}\\frac{G_{\\nu\\nu}G_{\\mu\\mu}G_{\\nu\\mu}+G_{\\nu\\mu}^3}\n{l_\\nu^2l_\\mu^2},\n\\quad G_{\\nu\\mu}=n_\\nu^tBn_\\mu.\n\\]\nAbreu’s formula yields the stated scalar identity. The Schur product theorem makes both summands nonnegative. For \\(k_\\nu=\\gamma_\\nu\\sqrt{l_\\nu}\\), \\(Pk=0\\), so \\(I-P\\ge kk^t/\\|k\\|^2\\), giving both bounds.</p><p>Use the positive dependence \\((1,1,2,1,1)\\) for the full polygon and \\(n_1+n_2=0\\) on long tips. Active facet columns limit to unit vectors and active smooth-corner columns to orthonormal vectors. For three-direction satellites, the rank-one matrices of the three distinct directions are linearly independent, so the second numerator is positive, including smooth axes.</p><p>For homogeneous forms and moment Euler field \\(E\\),\n\\[\nR^2=2\\sum l_\\nu,\\qquad 2E=R\\partial_R.\n\\]\nThus \\(g=dR^2+R^2h\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Scal}(h)\\ge6\\). Tapering the redundant \\((p+v)/4\\) Hessian term to zero gives the round quotient link through the same nonnegative cone calculation.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.3", "orig": "Lemma 6.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For shrink parameter \\(\\delta=r_i\\) or \\(h_i=L/4-r_i\\), there are homogeneous cone models with scale-invariant estimates \\( \\|g-g_c\\|_{C^k(g_c)} \\le C_k(\\delta/R^2+R^2), \\qquad C\\sqrt\\delta\\le R\\le R_*. \\) Rescaling …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For shrink parameter \\(\\delta=r_i\\) or \\(h_i=L/4-r_i\\), there are homogeneous cone models with scale-invariant estimates\n\\[\n\\|g-g_c\\|_{C^k(g_c)}\n\\le C_k(\\delta/R^2+R^2),\n\\qquad C\\sqrt\\delta\\le R\\le R_*.\n\\]\nRescaling local moments by \\(\\delta\\) gives \\(g=\\delta(g_{\\rm sat}+o(1))\\). Conformal changes produce fixed-size asymptotically cylindrical positive-scalar ends, admitting arbitrarily deep exact productization with vanishing errors.</p>", "proof": "<p>At high shrink use \\(p=\\xi+4y'\\), \\(v=L-\\xi\\), with forms \\(p,v,(p+v)/4-h_1\\). At low shrink use \\(q=\\xi-4y'\\), \\(l=r_1+y'\\), with forms \\(q,l,q+8l-8r_1\\). Only a redundant homogeneous denominator is shifted, producing \\(O(\\delta/R^2)\\); regular terms produce \\(O(R^2)\\). Rescaled annuli and square-root facet coordinates give every derivative estimate through axes and uniformly on long tips.</p><p>Choose\n\\[\nF(t)=\\int_t^\\infty\\chi_\\infty(s)s^{-2}ds,\\qquad\nf=1+c\\chi_0(R)\\delta^{-1/2}F(R/\\sqrt\\delta).\n\\]\nFor \\(q(R)=\\delta^{-1/2}F(R/\\sqrt\\delta)\\),\n\\[\n\\Delta q=\n-\\frac{\\chi_\\infty'(R/\\sqrt\\delta)|dR|^2}{\\sqrt\\delta R^2}\n+\\frac{\\chi_\\infty(R/\\sqrt\\delta)}{R^3}\n(2|dR|^2-R\\Delta R).\n\\]\nThe cone has \\(R\\Delta R=3|dR|^2\\). A large initial ramp and small \\(R_*\\) make \\(-\\Delta q&gt;0\\); strict compact positivity absorbs outer-cutoff errors for small \\(c\\). Treat the original puncture similarly, inside a low satellite if necessary. Its nested scale cancels under four-dimensional conformal scaling.</p><p>With \\(s=-\\log R\\), the limiting metric is \\(c^2(ds^2+h)\\). Deep fixed-width interpolation to that exact product preserves positive scalar curvature.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.4", "orig": "Proposition 6.7 and Lemma 6.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The local metrics assemble over the original two-interval macro square and extend across the low-low corner without an extra face.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The local metrics assemble over the original two-interval macro square and extend across the low-low corner without an extra face. All unbounded regions are exact rational products, asymptotically positive products, or the specified spherical tubes; all other rescaled truncations have compact smooth geometry. Bulk errors integrate to \\(o(\\epsilon^{-1})\\), uniformly under deeper regularization and longer auxiliary rational collars.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use \\(t_i\\in[-3,3]\\), with true \\(J_i\\) at \\(-3\\), lens face at \\(3\\), and nondecreasing \\(\\alpha_i\\) equal to zero for \\(t_i\\le0\\), \\(1/4\\) for \\(t_i\\ge1\\). For \\(w_0=\\alpha_1+\\alpha_2\\ge3\\epsilon\\), put\n\\[\na_i=\\alpha_i+dw_0(1-8\\alpha_i).\n\\]\nAt either saturation the shrink parameter is \\(d/(1+d(2-8w_0))\\). Productize at \\(R\\asymp d^{1/4}\\). Insert genuine lens length only inside that exact collar. At low saturation retain the subchain collar while changing its exterior to introduce the round \\(J_i\\), possible because the subchain misses \\(J_i\\). Fix the far side before insertion, ensuring parameter locality. Compatible nested and disjoint operations commute.</p><p>Face-preserving polygon diffeomorphisms place all marked interfaces on one fixed plumbing. Shorten obsolete products before convex interpolation on bounded exterior pieces.</p><p>For small width set \\(z=y'+r_1\\). The strip limit is\n\\[\n\\frac{dz^2}{2z(1-z)}+2z(1-z)d\\theta_y^2\n+\\tfrac12d\\xi^2+2d\\theta_x^2,\n\\]\nwhose first part is the round \\(S^2(1/\\sqrt2)\\). Make a long bulk strip exact, fix its length comparable to \\(\\epsilon^{-1}\\), and interpolate bounded tips to templates retaining both \\(J_i\\)-collars. Below \\(w_0=\\epsilon\\) keep only these templates and the tube.</p><p>The shrink identities exclude additional degeneration types. For fixed truncation \\(T\\), error costs have the form\n\\[\nC_T+\\eta(T)C\\epsilon^{-1},\\qquad \\eta(T)\\to0.\n\\]\nMultiply by \\(\\epsilon\\), first send \\(\\epsilon\\to0\\), then \\(T\\to\\infty\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 7.5", "orig": "Lemmas 6.9–6.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every nonzero-weight vertex in the convex expansion of \\(H\\) among \\( U,\\quad U+X_1/4,\\quad U+X_2/4,\\quad U+(X_1+X_2)/4 \\) is supported rationally on the retained smooth main piece.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nonzero-weight vertex in the convex expansion of \\(H\\) among\n\\[\nU,\\quad U+X_1/4,\\quad U+X_2/4,\\quad U+(X_1+X_2)/4\n\\]\nis supported rationally on the retained smooth main piece. Any side used in a nonempty vertex remains a whole-sphere test.</p><p>For every fixed finite collection of real classes \\(\\Lambda\\), there are closed comparison forms \\(b_*/(2\\pi i)\\) representing them, equal on bulk tubes to\n\\[\nb_*=i(\\Lambda U)\\operatorname{vol}_{S^2(1/\\sqrt2)},\n\\]\nand zero on long rational middles, such that\n\\[\n\\int|b_*|^2=O(\\epsilon^{-1}),\\qquad\n\\int_{\\rm off\\ tubes}|b_*|^2=o(\\epsilon^{-1}).\n\\]\nThe squared negative scalar-curvature cost is likewise \\(o(\\epsilon^{-1})\\) after omitting \\(Y\\)-middles and retaining prefixes of length \\(O(\\epsilon^{-1/2})\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>With \\(p=4a,q=4b\\), vertex weights are \\((1-p)(1-q),p(1-q),(1-p)q,pq\\). A zero coefficient removes that side from every used vertex; a coefficient one makes every used vertex orthogonal to the collapsed divisor. Rational Mayer–Vietoris gives compactly supported representatives on the retained piece. Positive coefficients lie in the whole-test range.</p><p>Shorten long products and truncate all smooth and satellite pieces. There are finitely many compact smooth decomposition types. Choose basis representatives there; subtract cutoff primitives to set them to zero on rational middles and to the prescribed vertical flux on tube middles. Restore products with these tangential forms. Parameter partitions of unity preserve closure and class. Torsion restrictions retain their flat line references.</p><p>Two-form energy is invariant under four-dimensional conformal changes and rescaling. Bounded transitions contribute \\(C_T\\), rational middles contribute zero, and tube errors have the preceding two-stage bound. Positive product regions have no negative scalar cost; finite \\(Y\\)-prefixes cost \\(O(\\epsilon^{-1/2})\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Theorem 7.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Finite auxiliary choices give, simultaneously at all genuine faces:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the finite problem and an upper bound on total topological charge, including nonnegative ideal losses.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(b=i\\beta\\), \\([\\beta/(2\\pi)]=\\Lambda\\), and \\([D/(2\\pi)]=v\\) at a parallel reduction.</p><p class=\"li\">Type \\(R\\) means zero spinor and a connection with continuous stabilizer; type \\(S\\) means a parallel splitting carrying a nonzero spinor in its active line.</p><p class=\"li\">On a positive bridge set\n\\[\nu=vU,\\quad \\lambda=\\Lambda U=-1-e_j+e_{j+1},\n\\quad H_I=U+\\tfrac14\\sum_{i\\in I}X_i,\\quad z_I=vH_I.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\n\\lambda_1=-\\tfrac32+e_{j+1},\\quad\n\\lambda_2=-\\tfrac12-e_j,\\quad \\lambda_{12}=-1,\\quad\nu\\equiv\\lambda\\pmod2.\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Finite auxiliary choices give, simultaneously at all genuine faces:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. Either \\(u=0\\), or some nonempty set \\(I\\) of adjacent whole sides satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{sign}(u)z_I\\le0\\quad(R),\\qquad\n\\operatorname{sign}(u)z_I&lt;|\\lambda_I|\\quad(S).\n\\]</p><p class=\"li num\">2. No \\(R\\)-component contains an outside cap. Each old cap \\(W\\), before its separate extra blowup, has a fixed constant \\(C_W\\) such that\n\\[\nv_W^2\\le C_W,\\qquad(\\Lambda_W+v_W)^2\\le C_W\n\\]\non an \\(S\\)-component. The constant is independent of stack length and new exceptional evaluation.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. Separated lens backgrounds are harmonic ASD, and complementary data agree under the lens-bit flip.</p><p>A positive tolerance for ordinary gradient perturbations is chosen before arbitrary sufficiently long finite \\(Y\\)-lengths; further nongradient tolerances may depend on those selected finite lengths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 8.1–8.4 prove the simultaneous bridge inequalities and compatible harmonic backgrounds. Lemma 8.5 gives old-cap constants and the required quantifier order. On separated lens caps retain the positive metric, ASD background, zero Dirac perturbation, and only small compact ASD perturbations. The exterior backgrounds depend only on restricted classes and therefore agree under the supported bit change.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.1", "orig": "Lemma 7.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On a completed piece with rational-homology-sphere ends, each real degree-two class has a unique \\(L^2\\) harmonic representative.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On a completed piece with rational-homology-sphere ends, each real degree-two class has a unique \\(L^2\\) harmonic representative. Under bounded norms, long rational-neck harmonic forms converge exponentially on truncations to the restricted representatives. Backgrounds can be flattened on finite \\(Y\\)-middles and made product-compatible at genuine faces. On positive toric pieces their self-dual parts are proportional to the Kähler form; on separated lens caps they are ASD.</p>", "proof": "<p>Relative-to-absolute degree-two cohomology is an isomorphism over \\(\\mathbb R\\). Minimize norm among same-class closed representatives. Orthogonal projection gives the harmonic form; a decaying primitive and integration by parts give uniqueness.</p><p>On a rational cylinder, tangential one- and two-form Laplacians have a positive gap. Each mode satisfies \\(-f''+\\mu f=0\\), \\(\\mu\\ge\\mu_0&gt;0\\), yielding exponential interior bounds and primitives. Cut-and-glue comparison representatives and the projection identity\n\\[\n\\|h_T-b_T\\|_2^2=\\|h_T\\|_2^2-\\|b_T\\|_2^2\n\\]\ngive exponential harmonic gluing. Product-weight averaging over subsets of long genuine collars preserves closure, class, and face locality. Flatten using the small primitives and fixed torsion flat connections.</p><p>The Kähler form has finite two-form energy and remains self-dual harmonic under conformal completion. Since the positive main piece has \\(b^+=1\\), it spans that harmonic subspace. A lens cap has \\(b^+=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.2", "orig": "Lemmas 7.3–7.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For \\(\\zeta=f^2\\), \\(0\\le f\\le1\\), compactly supported …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">The equations are\n\\[\nD_a\\Phi=P_D,\\qquad\n\\rho(F_a^{0,+})=(\\Phi\\Phi^*)_{00}+P_+.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(V=\\operatorname{Scal}/4+\\rho(b)/2\\), \\(h=\\max(0,-\\lambda_{\\min}V)\\), \\(d_*=1/4\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(\\zeta=f^2\\), \\(0\\le f\\le1\\), compactly supported,\n\\[\n\\frac12\\int\\zeta^2|\\nabla_a\\Phi|^2+\n\\frac{d_*}{4}\\int\\zeta^2|\\Phi|^4\n\\le\n2\\int\\zeta^2|P_D|^2+\n\\frac1{d_*}\\int\\zeta^2(h^2+|P_+|^2)\n+\\frac9{d_*}\\int\\frac{|d\\zeta|^4}{\\zeta^2}.\n\\]\nNo derivative of \\(P_D\\) occurs.</p><p>On a flat-background finite \\(Y\\)-middle with a bounded gradient perturbation \\(W\\) and additional errors of total squared cost \\(\\nu\\), suitable boundary slices satisfy\n\\[\n\\kappa_{\\rm middle}\\ge-C_Y-C_Y\\nu,\n\\]\nindependently of length.</p>", "proof": "<p>Pair \\(D_a^*D_a\\Phi=D_a^*P_D\\) with \\(\\zeta^2\\Phi\\). The quadratic term obeys\n\\[\n\\langle(\\Phi\\Phi^*)_{00}\\Phi,\\Phi\\rangle\n=\\tfrac14(s^4+t^4)+\\tfrac52s^2t^2\\ge\\tfrac14|\\Phi|^4.\n\\]\nBound derivative cross terms by Young’s inequality and absorb quartic portions. Since \\(|d(f^2)|^4/(f^2)^2=16|df|^4\\), the cutoff term is defined continuously.</p><p>In temporal gauge on the \\(Y\\)-middle,\n\\[\n\\dot\\Phi+D_a\\Phi=e,\\qquad\n\\dot a+*F_a=q(\\Phi)+\\sigma\\nabla W+r.\n\\]\nFor \\(\\mathcal H=\\langle D_a\\Phi,\\Phi\\rangle\\), the paper’s Clifford convention gives\n\\[\n-\\langle*F_a,\\dot a\\rangle\n=\\|\\dot a\\|^2+\\tfrac12\\mathcal H'\n+\\|\\dot\\Phi\\|^2-\\sigma W'\n-\\langle r,\\dot a\\rangle-\\langle e,\\dot\\Phi\\rangle.\n\\]\nLocal strip estimates select slices with \\(|\\mathcal H|\\le B_Y(1+\\nu)^{3/4}\\). Integrate and absorb the error pairings. Bounded endpoint \\(W\\)-values and \\((1+\\nu)^{3/4}\\le1+\\nu\\) give the charge bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.3", "orig": "Proposition 7.5 and Corollary 7.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For the retained region \\(\\mathcal R_\\epsilon\\), omitting \\(Y\\)-middles but retaining prefixes of length \\(C\\epsilon^{-1/2}\\) …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For the retained region \\(\\mathcal R_\\epsilon\\), omitting \\(Y\\)-middles but retaining prefixes of length \\(C\\epsilon^{-1/2}\\),\n\\[\n\\sum_T\\|\\beta-\\lambda_T\\omega_{S^2}\\|_{L^2(T)}^2+\n\\|\\beta\\|_{L^2(\\rm rest)}^2=o(\\epsilon^{-1}),\n\\qquad \\|\\beta\\|_2^2=O(\\epsilon^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor solutions of bounded total charge, bounded ordinary gradients, and additional squared errors \\(o(\\epsilon^{-1})\\),\n\\[\n\\int_{\\mathcal R_\\epsilon}\n\\bigl(|\\Phi|^4+|F_a^{0,+}|^2+|F_a^0|^2\\bigr)\n=o(\\epsilon^{-1}).\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(\\epsilon\\), corresponding finite bounds are independent of subsequent rational lengths and omitted \\(Y\\)-lengths. Every reduction in the small-width region has \\(vU=0\\) for sufficiently small \\(\\epsilon\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 7’s comparison form has energy\n\\[\n\\sum_T\\lambda_T^2\\operatorname{Vol}(T)+o(\\epsilon^{-1}).\n\\]\nFiberwise flux and Cauchy–Schwarz give the matching lower bound for harmonic energy, and on exact tubes\n\\[\n\\|\\beta-\\lambda_T\\omega_{S^2}\\|^2\n=\\|\\beta\\|^2-\\lambda_T^2\\operatorname{Vol}(T).\n\\]\nDiscard bounded tips and a small end fraction, then use uniform bulk convergence.</p><p>The tube has scalar curvature four and \\(|\\lambda_T|\\le2\\), so \\(\\operatorname{Scal}/4\\ge\\|\\rho(i\\lambda_T\\omega_{S^2})\\|/2\\). Hence the integrated negative zeroth-order cost is lower order. Apply Lemma 8.2 for spinor and self-dual curvature bounds. Summing the omitted-middle lower charge bounds gives\n\\[\n\\kappa(\\mathcal R_\\epsilon)\n\\le Q+q_YC_Y(1+\\nu).\n\\]\nUse \\(\\|F_a^0\\|_2^2=2\\|F_a^{0,+}\\|_2^2+8\\pi^2\\kappa\\); no charge sign is assumed on arbitrary main components.</p><p>A reduction has \\(|F_a^0|^2=|D|^2/2\\). Nonzero integral flux \\(u=vU\\) along a tube of length at least \\(c/\\epsilon\\) forces energy at least \\(c'u^2/\\epsilon\\), contradicting the lower-order bound.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.4", "orig": "Lemmas 7.7–7.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On an isolated positive-width main limit with harmonic background, an unperturbed reduction satisfies \\( vH=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad (vH)((\\Lambda+v)H)<0, \\) with the first case for zero spinor and the strict alternative …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On an isolated positive-width main limit with harmonic background, an unperturbed reduction satisfies\n\\[\nvH=0\\quad\\text{or}\\quad (vH)((\\Lambda+v)H)&lt;0,\n\\]\nwith the first case for zero spinor and the strict alternative for nonzero spinor. The vertex clamp of Proposition 8 persists for sufficiently small finite regularization, sufficiently long finite rational isolation, and sufficiently small perturbations, uniformly at genuine faces and long finite \\(Y\\)-seams.</p>", "proof": "<p>The reduced equations are\n\\[\n\\rho(iD^+)=(\\psi\\psi^*)_0.\n\\]\nThe integrated Dirac identity is\n\\[\n0=\\int\\left(|\\nabla\\psi|^2+\\frac{\\operatorname{Scal}}4|\\psi|^2\\right)\n+2\\int\\bigl(\\langle\\beta^+,D^+\\rangle+|D^+|^2\\bigr).\n\\]\nFinite-energy cutoffs justify integration. The first integral is strictly positive for \\(\\psi\\ne0\\), by nonnegative scalar curvature and positivity on an open set. Projecting to the one-dimensional positive harmonic space gives\n\\[\n\\int(\\langle\\beta^+,D^+\\rangle+|D^+|^2)\n=(2\\pi)^2\\frac{(vH)((\\Lambda+v)H)}{H^2}\n+\\|D_\\perp\\|^2.\n\\]</p><p>Expand \\(H\\) with its four nonnegative vertex weights. All nonempty vertex \\(\\Lambda\\)-values are negative, and parity gives \\(|u|\\ge|\\lambda|\\) for \\(u\\ne0\\). Failure of every available vertex inequality contradicts the displayed alternative.</p><p>For finite persistence, take a hypothetical failing sequence. Lemma 8.3 bounds retained energy and excludes small width. Abelian Coulomb gauge and the local estimate give weak convergence; logarithmic cutoffs with \\(\\int|df_R|^4\\le C|\\log R|^{-3}\\) exclude spinor atoms. Strong \\(L^4\\), then elliptic bootstrap, gives an unperturbed reduced limit. Only positive-weight vertices are needed; they have compact dual representatives on the retained piece. Their evaluations lie in a discrete lattice and become constant along a subsequence. The same vertex contradiction applies, even if the limiting spinor vanishes.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 8.5", "orig": "Proposition 7.9 and Corollary 7.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Each old cap admits a fixed metric and constant \\(C_W\\) with the asserted independence from exterior choices.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Each old cap admits a fixed metric and constant \\(C_W\\) with the asserted independence from exterior choices. For every fixed finite exterior problem there are finite isolation and smallness thresholds enforcing the cap bounds and excluding cap-containing \\(R\\). These constants can be fixed before delays, exceptional evaluations, and repetitions. The final gradient tolerance precedes the finite \\(Y\\)-lengths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Every allowed line difference is nonzero because it evaluates oddly on the old square-zero \\(A\\). The old cap has \\(b^+&gt;0\\). Compactly supported metric variation moves every nonzero integral ASD wall: unique continuation makes nonzero ASD and self-dual harmonic forms overlap in the permitted variation region. Baire’s theorem avoids countably many walls.</p><p>Fix the harmonic background and an \\(L^2\\)-orthonormal self-dual harmonic basis \\(\\omega_a\\), with exponentially decreasing end primitives. The local spinor estimate bounds \\(\\|D^+\\|\\) on every unit strip by a cap-only constant. Summing its pairings with the decreasing \\(\\omega_a\\) bounds \\(\\|v_W^+\\|\\) by a cap-only \\(B_W\\).</p><p>For each fixed exterior problem, retained energy bounds the finite set of possible integral restrictions. To identify the positive projection, use the compactly supported representatives\n\\[\n\\widehat\\omega_{a,N}=\\chi_N\\omega_a+d\\chi_N\\wedge\\alpha_a.\n\\]\nPrefix curvature is bounded by \\(E_{\\mathcal P,\\epsilon}+C_W'(N+2)\\); therefore the correction pairing is bounded by\n\\[\nC_W'e^{-\\sigma N}\\sqrt{E_{\\mathcal P,\\epsilon}+C_W'(N+2)}\\to0.\n\\]\nAn \\(R\\)-limit contradicts the wall choice. For \\(S\\),\n\\[\nv_W^2\\le B_W^2,\\qquad\n(\\Lambda_W+v_W)^2\\le(\\|\\Lambda_W^+\\|+B_W)^2.\n\\]\nAdd a strict cushion to define \\(C_W\\), and obtain finite thresholds by contradiction.</p><p>After the exterior topology is fixed, choose \\(\\epsilon\\), then regularization and isolation. A diagonal failure with gradients tending to zero, isolations increasing, and all \\(Y\\)-lengths increasing would contradict Lemma 8.4 or the cap argument. This supplies one positive gradient tolerance before those lengths. Only after fixing finite lengths choose further nongradient errors with sufficiently small total squared cost.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Theorem 4.1", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are fixed caps \\(C_-,C_+\\) with rational boundary \\(Y_2\\), fixed determinant data trivialized there, and finite integral point and surface probes such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are fixed caps \\(C_-,C_+\\) with rational boundary \\(Y_2\\), fixed determinant data trivialized there, and finite integral point and surface probes such that:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H_1(C_\\pm;\\mathbb Z)=0\\), \\(b^+(C_\\pm)&gt;0\\);</p><p class=\"li\">each contains a square-zero genus-two \\(A\\) with determinant evaluation one and a tight genus-two surface of square two;</p><p class=\"li\">each has a separate extra exceptional summand, determinant evaluation zero, with all probes in the old part;</p><p class=\"li\">ordinary cut counts give a rational cycle \\(x\\) and cocycle \\(y\\) with \\(yx\\ne0\\), and fixed integral multiples are integral;</p><p class=\"li\">probe degree \\(z\\) is fixed, and old-cap periods exclude reducers in every bounded range needed later, with the finite isolation extensions of Proposition 8.</p><p>Recomputing at the later permitted cap data gives the same nonzero closed pairing, up to fixed normalization and orientation.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use Lemmas 9.1–9.2 to obtain a nonzero closure with an internal rational seam. Lemma 9.3 constructs the required caps, extra exceptional summands, probes, and nonzero pairing. Clear the fixed denominators to obtain integral cap states. Their total probe degree is fixed.</p><p>At later permitted cap data, stretch the same old closed pairing. Its Donaldson invariant is unchanged because \\(b^+&gt;1\\); Lemma 5.5 supplies the exact-test comparison, and Proposition 8 supplies the renewed cap exclusion. This preserves the pairing without asserting separate metric independence of each cap state.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.1", "orig": "Proposition 4.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "On \\(C_L=C(Y_0)\\otimes L_a\\), there are a differential \\(d_L\\), cycle \\(u\\), cocycle \\(v\\), and chain map \\(T_L\\) with \\(T_L\\bmod J=T\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Label the \\(a\\) surface occurrences in Proposition 5 separately, with disjoint parallel meridian intersections \\(\\gamma_i\\subset Y_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Join the labels across each bridge \\(T\\) using relative surfaces in its simply connected halves.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n\\[\nL_a=\\mathbb Q[t_1,\\ldots,t_a]/(t_1^2,\\ldots,t_a^2),\\qquad J=(t_1,\\ldots,t_a).\n\\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>On \\(C_L=C(Y_0)\\otimes L_a\\), there are a differential \\(d_L\\), cycle \\(u\\), cocycle \\(v\\), and chain map \\(T_L\\) with \\(T_L\\bmod J=T\\). The all-label coefficient of \\(vT_L^ku\\) is the corresponding fixed-determinant closed Donaldson invariant on the closure with \\(T^k\\) inserted, up to fixed nonzero normalization. In particular,\n\\[\n[t_1\\cdots t_a](vu)\\ne0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(P\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,a\\}\\), count cylinder trajectories with one moving exact test on each \\(\\mathbb R\\times\\gamma_i\\), \\(i\\in P\\), and set \\(d_L=\\sum_Pt_Pd_P\\). Parametrized trajectory index is \\(1+2|P|\\). Define cap and bridge coefficients similarly, omitting marks for absent labels.</p><p>The compactified one-dimensional identities distribute each label to exactly one broken factor:\n\\[\n\\sum_{P_1\\sqcup P_2=P}d_{P_1}d_{P_2}=0.\n\\]\nThey give \\(d_L^2=0\\), \\(d_Lu=0\\), \\(vd_L=0\\), and \\(d_LT_L=T_Ld_L\\). Even insertion degrees give no extra Koszul signs and distinct labels give no factorials.</p><p>Stretch admissible seams. Proposition 2 excludes particles and unwanted constant levels, and ordinary gluing gives the coefficient expansion. Lemma 5.5 identifies the closed counts. At \\(k=0\\) the all-label coefficient is \\(4^{a+m}D_{X_0}^w(x_0^mh^a)\\), up to sign, and is nonzero.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.2", "orig": "Lemma 4.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For some \\(k\\ge1\\), \\( [t_1\\cdots t_a](vT_L^ku)\\ne0. \\)", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For some \\(k\\ge1\\),\n\\[\n[t_1\\cdots t_a](vT_L^ku)\\ne0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Filter the mapping cone of \\(T_L\\) by powers of \\(J\\). Its associated graded is a finite sum of rational cones of \\(T\\), acyclic by Proposition 3. Hence \\(T_L\\) induces an automorphism \\(F\\) of the finite-dimensional rational homology.</p><p>If \\(a_0+\\cdots+a_Ns^N\\) is its characteristic polynomial, \\(a_0\\ne0\\), so\n\\[\n\\operatorname{id}=-a_0^{-1}\\sum_{j=1}^Na_jF^j.\n\\]\nApply \\([t_1\\cdots t_a]\\,v(\\,\\cdot\\,)u\\). Its identity value is nonzero, so some positive-power value is nonzero. No freeness over \\(L_a\\) is needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 9.3", "orig": "Proposition 4.11", "depth": 1, "snippet": "After allocating all probes into the two sides of an internal rational \\(Y_2\\)-seam, generic old-cap periods, sufficiently long finite exceptional isolation, and sufficiently small ordinary data give irreducible cap …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After allocating all probes into the two sides of an internal rational \\(Y_2\\)-seam, generic old-cap periods, sufficiently long finite exceptional isolation, and sufficiently small ordinary data give irreducible cap cycles whose pairing is the nonzero allocated invariant. The relevant cap charge ranges and prefix bounds are uniform in outgoing Floer lengths.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the nonzero positive-power closure, rational Mayer–Vietoris splits \\(H_2\\) across \\(Y_2\\). Multilinearity gives a nonzero allocation of all probes to cap interiors. Clear denominators and choose embedded representatives with the prescribed general position. Blow up one point in each cap away from probes, with zero determinant evaluation; the pullback blowup factor has constant term one.</p><p>A reducer has odd line-difference evaluation on \\(A\\). Proposition 8’s cap periods exclude it in the required bounded ranges. For cap probe degree \\(z_W\\), only indices \\(z_W,z_W+1\\), and their one-parameter comparisons occur. The APS formula\n\\[\ni=8\\kappa+b_W(\\alpha)\n\\]\nbounds charge: the finitely many end corrections remain uniformly bounded for small Floer data. Gradient endpoint errors telescope along outgoing chains, bounding compact-cap charge and giving prefix bounds \\(E_{\\mathcal P}+C_W\\delta^2N\\).</p><p>Particles are excluded by Proposition 2. At a central seam, matching contributes three, while regular irreducible cut cap dimensions are nonnegative. A maximal central cylinder group has the same surplus; in cap-cycle identities it also requires a nonconstant outgoing trajectory. Thus only ordinary irreducible breaks remain, giving cycles and cocycles. Long-neck gluing identifies their pairing with the allocated closed invariant, without a central multiplicity.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Theorem 5.11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "There are arbitrarily large finite stacks, with positive bridges at spacing four, such that:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the caps and probes of Proposition 9.</p><p class=\"li\">Prescribe lower bounds on both outside pads and arbitrary odd values of \\(\\Lambda\\) on the separate extra exceptional spheres.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the lifts of Proposition 6 and the locally signed map \\(B\\) of Proposition 4.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are arbitrarily large finite stacks, with positive bridges at spacing four, such that:\n\\[\nD_I=n+z,\\qquad \\Omega\\ne0,\\qquad n_D=\\Theta-\\kappa&gt;0.\n\\]\nHere \\(\\Omega\\) is the actual oriented ordinary family count summed over determinant bits with the fixed local weights. All auxiliary collars are finite, and the data satisfy Propositions 7–8. Topology and lifts are chosen before auxiliary sizes, perturbation tolerances, and external Floer gluing lengths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Clear cap denominators and let their integral pairing be \\(q\\ne0\\). Choose \\(N\\) with \\(q\\not\\equiv0\\pmod{2^N}\\), and let \\(L\\) be the positive-rank free Floer lattice of \\(Y_2\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nA_{\\rm ret}=\\varphi_*B,\\qquad D_{\\rm ret}=HB.\n\\]\nThey are automorphisms of \\(L/2^NL\\) by Lemma 10.4. The four-interval pattern has operator \\(A_{\\rm ret}^3D_{\\rm ret}\\). Choose a common exponent \\(E\\), divisible by eight. Choose both pads as sufficiently large multiples of \\(E\\), and repeat the pattern \\(q_0\\) times, with \\(q_0\\) a sufficiently large multiple of \\(E\\). Then\n\\[\nm=q_0,\\qquad n=p_-+4q_0+p_+,\n\\]\nand the entire operation is the identity modulo \\(2^N\\).</p><p>Check bundle degree before choosing metrics. The base determinant square equals that of the old cap pairing; positive rank increases by \\(m\\), so required \\(8\\kappa\\) changes by \\(n+3m\\), divisible by eight. All bit sectors are integral. Proposition 6 gives\n\\[\nn_D=\\frac{q_0}{8}+\\Theta_0-\n\\frac{p_-+p_++z+3+3b_0}{8}&gt;0\n\\]\nfor large allowed \\(q_0\\).</p><p>Choose periodic factor data below the tolerances, then finite external gluing lengths. Lemma 10.3 identifies the actual count:\n\\[\n\\Omega\\equiv\\pm q\\not\\equiv0\\pmod{2^N}.\n\\]\nFurther small closed generic changes preserve this integer and need not be periodic.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "Lemmas 5.5–5.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Sphere representatives preserve \\(B\\), avoid \\(-1\\) for a commuting whole-sphere evaluation \\(vS_i=0\\), and switch near \\(J_i\\) to compact half-sphere events.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Sphere representatives preserve \\(B\\), avoid \\(-1\\) for a commuting whole-sphere evaluation \\(vS_i=0\\), and switch near \\(J_i\\) to compact half-sphere events. At a lens face they are tensor-paired and cap-supported.</p><p>After virtually restoring lens caps using differences \\(0,\\pm4,\\pm2\\), a negative reducible piece with \\(s\\) assigned tests satisfies\n\\[\n8\\kappa_{\\rm piece}\\ge4s.\n\\]\nA reducible piece containing a positive cell has\n\\[\n\\kappa_{\\rm piece}\\in\\tfrac12\\mathbb Z,\\qquad\n\\kappa_{\\rm piece}\\ge\\tfrac12.\n\\]\nThese bounds persist in the small-data finite templates and long-neck comparisons.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the zero-winding modification and segmentation of Proposition 2. Their homotopies satisfy Proposition 4’s invariance conditions, fix the nonzero-winding minimum lens paths, and preserve tensor pairing.</p><p>A restored reduction has\n\\[\n\\kappa=-v^2/4+\\ell,\\qquad \\ell\\ge0\\text{ integral}.\n\\]\nNegative half evaluations are even. A nonzero one contributes at least two to \\(-v^2\\); a test with no such half requires a distinct loss unit. Thus each test costs at least four in \\(8\\kappa\\).</p><p>On the positive cell, \\(vG\\) is even and precisely two exceptional evaluations are odd, making \\(v^2\\) even. Smallness gives nonnegative half-integral charge. Zero is impossible because an odd-\\(w_2\\) sphere \\(\\pm(T_2-T_1)\\), away from all varying collars, would acquire a flat projective limit. Gradient energy identities make this argument independent of internal collar length.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.2", "orig": "Proposition 5.7 and Lemma 5.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The two-parameter, two-test macro \\(BHB\\), with irreducible outer limits and actual index two, defines an integral homology map equal to the composition \\(BHB\\), independent of genuine-face-preserving interpolation, and …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The two-parameter, two-test macro \\(BHB\\), with irreducible outer limits and actual index two, defines an integral homology map equal to the composition \\(BHB\\), independent of genuine-face-preserving interpolation, and a mod-two unit.</p><p>Allowing central outer limits, a single interval has \\(i_{\\rm loc}-1\\ge0\\). A macro with \\(a\\) central outer limits satisfies\n\\[\ni_{\\rm loc}-2\\ge-2a.\n\\]\nWith irreducible outer limits, zero excess occurs only for ordinary rigid solutions without true faces, particles, or positive trajectory losses.</p>", "proof": "<p>With \\(k\\) primary cuts and homotopy count \\(t\\in\\{0,1\\}\\), retain \\(\\sum p=2-k\\), \\(\\sum s=2\\). Irreducibles cost \\(2s-p\\), a separated lens has positive extra cost, and particles have net loss at least four. A negative reducible group with both exterior central matches costs at least \\(4s_R+3\\), giving total at least\n\\[\n4s_R+3+2(2-s_R)-(2-k)-t\\ge5+k-t&gt;3-t.\n\\]\nA group containing the positive reducer costs at least four. For \\(k=0\\), two true-end trajectories give six; for \\(k=1\\), the test assignments give at least \\(5-t\\) or six; for \\(k=2\\), at least \\(4-t\\). Thus only irreducibles and a single paired minimum lens boundary remain. The lens pair cancels integrally.</p><p>Stretch all internal \\(Y_{-2},Y_0,Y_2\\)-lengths. The increasing halves have no positive direction, as their orthogonal trace forms show, and no reducer meets admissible \\(Y_0\\). The same budget leaves exactly the product \\(BHB\\).</p><p>For the central-limit estimate put \\(b=1\\) or \\(2\\). Index addition gives\n\\[\ni_{\\rm loc}-b=\\sum_{\\rm pieces}(i+p-2s)+4k.\n\\]\nIrreducible scores are nonnegative, negative reducer scores are at least \\(-3+2s+p\\), and a positive reducer’s score is at least \\(-2+p-2s\\). Enumerating \\(k=0,1,2\\) and assignments gives the paper’s minima: for a positive reducer they are \\(-4\\) at \\(k=0\\); \\(1,-1,0,-4\\) at \\(k=1\\); and \\(2,-1,-4\\) according to the number of reducible outer pieces at \\(k=2\\). Each is at least \\(-2a\\); when \\(a=0\\), every primary face is strict.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.3", "orig": "Proposition 5.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For any fixed finite stack and data satisfying Proposition 8, sufficiently long finite external negative \\(Y\\)-necks identify the actual closed count with the ordinary composition of cap states, intervals, and macro …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For any fixed finite stack and data satisfying Proposition 8, sufficiently long finite external negative \\(Y\\)-necks identify the actual closed count with the ordinary composition of cap states, intervals, and macro maps, up to one overall sign. The conclusion persists under sufficiently small further genuine-face-preserving changes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the bounded cap ranges and Proposition 8’s finite isolation thresholds to exclude cap reducers uniformly during external stretching. Recompute the old nonzero pairing at the final cap data as in Proposition 9.</p><p>Let \\(S_c\\) count external seams containing a central state, and let \\(A_c\\) count macro central outer limits. A central seam contributes at least three. By macro separation and outside pads, each such seam is adjacent to at most one macro, so \\(A_c\\le S_c\\). Lemma 10.2 gives total excess at least\n\\[\n3S_c-2A_c\\ge S_c.\n\\]\nThe total excess is zero. Hence no central seam occurs. Every factor then has nonnegative excess, and equality excludes genuine faces, particles, nonconstant seam trajectories, and other positive losses. Ordinary gluing gives precisely the rigid product.</p><p>Order determinant lines and cuts in cobordism order. Fixed factor parities make the comparison sign independent of solutions. Local flip comparisons commute with all gluings. Proposition 8 allows a gradient tolerance before external lengths; choose gradients below it, then lengths in the gluing range, then remaining nongradient errors. Compactness makes absence of extra limits and the count open at these finite choices.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.4", "orig": "Lemma 5.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "If a map of finite free integral complexes is a mod-two quasi-isomorphism, its map on free homology quotients is an isomorphism after localization at two, and hence modulo every \\(2^N\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>If a map of finite free integral complexes is a mod-two quasi-isomorphism, its map on free homology quotients is an isomorphism after localization at two, and hence modulo every \\(2^N\\). On a fixed rank-\\(r\\) quotient, all such automorphisms modulo \\(2^N\\) have orders dividing the exponent of \\(\\operatorname{GL}_r(\\mathbb Z/2^N)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The localized mapping-cone homology has zero quotient by the maximal ideal \\((2)\\); the universal coefficient sequence and Nakayama’s lemma make it zero. The induced free-quotient matrix has odd determinant, so the adjugate formula gives invertibility modulo \\(2^N\\). The finite general linear group has a common exponent. No torsion-freeness of integral homology is assumed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Proposition 8.3, Lemma 8.4, Corollary 8.5", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The framed corrections are …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(N\\) be the completed disk bundle over \\(S^2\\) with section \\(S^2=-4\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Normalize by a local determinant root. Boundary states are the central states \\(+\\), \\(-\\), and trace zero.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\kappa_N\\) include cap particles and all cylindrical trajectory charge, with the state seen from the main component.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the positive metric and harmonic ASD background, with \\(|\\Lambda S|=2\\), zero cap Dirac perturbation, and small permitted compact ASD perturbations.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The framed corrections are\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c|c}\n\\text{state}&amp;\\text{charge}&amp;\\delta_I&amp;\\delta_{\\rm sp}\\\\ \\hline\n+,-&amp;\\kappa_N\\in\\mathbb Z&amp;8\\kappa_N&amp;6\\kappa_N\\\\\n\\text{trace}&amp;\\kappa_N\\in\\frac14+\\mathbb Z&amp;\n2+8(\\kappa_N-\\frac14)&amp;2+6(\\kappa_N-\\frac14).\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nThe spinor vanishes on separated caps and cylinders. With the sphere test,\n\\[\n\\kappa_N\\ge1\\quad\\text{at central states},\\qquad\n\\kappa_N\\in\\tfrac14+\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\quad\\text{at trace}.\n\\]\nAt trace charge \\(1/4\\) there are no particles or nonconstant levels; the cap’s Dirac operator is invertible, its framed tangential ASD index is zero, its normal complex ASD index is one, and its normalized eigenline sweep has winding of absolute value one.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 11.1 establishes the full local index and reference table. Lemma 11.2 supplies the analytic vanishing and minimum-charge conclusions. Normal ASD surjectivity and tested cap regularity are not inferred from the index; they are constructed later.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.1", "orig": "Proposition 8.3, index calculation", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Split references have differences and charges \\( d=0,\\ \\kappa_{\\rm ref}=0\\quad(+);\\qquad d=\\pm4,\\ \\kappa_{\\rm ref}=1\\quad(-);\\qquad d=\\pm2,\\ \\kappa_{\\rm ref}=\\tfrac14\\quad(\\text{trace}). \\) Choose the active reference …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Split references have differences and charges\n\\[\nd=0,\\ \\kappa_{\\rm ref}=0\\quad(+);\\qquad\nd=\\pm4,\\ \\kappa_{\\rm ref}=1\\quad(-);\\qquad\nd=\\pm2,\\ \\kappa_{\\rm ref}=\\tfrac14\\quad(\\text{trace}).\n\\]\nChoose the active reference at \\(-\\) so \\(|\\Lambda S+d|\\le4\\), and at trace preserve the prescribed eigenline. Their pure-line tangential correction, including matching, is zero.</p>", "proof": "<p>A class of evaluation \\(r\\) has square \\(-r^2/4\\), so a split difference \\(d\\) has charge \\(d^2/16\\) and normalized boundary eigencharacter \\(\\exp(\\pi id/4)\\).</p><p>Contract the negative section of \\(\\mathbb F_4\\) to \\(\\mathbb P(1,1,4)\\). For fiber twist \\(\\mathcal O(aT)\\), the spin-c determinant evaluates as \\(k(S)=-2+2a\\), and the resolution Euler characteristic is \\(a+1\\). On the weighted plane,\n\\[\n\\chi(\\mathcal O(a))=H(a)+H(-a-6),\\qquad\nH(j)=\\sum_{b=0}^{\\lfloor j/4\\rfloor}(j-4b+1)\n\\]\nfor \\(j\\ge0\\), and zero otherwise. Finite-group invariant Dolbeault resolutions identify this with the orbifold Dirac index. The common-complement identification fixes the full boundary spin-c structure.</p><p>The quotient-ball Dirac index is zero by positive scalar curvature. The cap Dirac indices for \\(k(S)=-6,-4,-2,0,2,4,6\\) are\n\\[\n-1,0,0,0,0,0,-1.\n\\]\nThe reversed normal Dolbeault comparisons give complex ASD indices \\(0,1,4\\) for \\(d=0,2,4\\), hence real indices \\(0,2,8\\). The two coupled line indices sum to \\(0,0,-1\\), respectively. Increasing charge by one changes ASD index by eight and complex Dirac index by minus one, giving the correction table.</p><p>At \\(-\\), the two choices \\(d=\\pm4\\) differ by a degree-four line trivial on the lens boundary; choose the sign making \\(|K(S)|\\le4\\). The table gives zero active-line Dirac index and therefore zero tangential correction. Positive-scalar link homotopies preserve the index gaps.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 11.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.4 and Corollary 8.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The vanishing, tested charge bounds, and minimum-cap properties in Proposition 11 hold.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The vanishing, tested charge bounds, and minimum-cap properties in Proposition 11 hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>The Weitzenböck identity on the positive-scalar cap with ASD fixed-line curvature kills the spinor; sufficiently small permitted curvature perturbations are absorbed. The same applies to exact cylinders. Nonconstant ASD levels have positive charge. Chern–Weil and smallness give \\(\\kappa_{\\rm cap}&gt;-1/4\\), so discreteness gives nonnegative cap charge.</p><p>At \\(+\\), zero charge is flat and misses the modified sphere test; at \\(-\\), a flat extension is impossible because the cap is simply connected. These avoidances persist by small-data compactness. At effective trace charge \\(1/4\\), an integer particle is impossible and no nonconstant level can remain after paying for a tested cap.</p><p>Order the minimum difference by \\(d=2\\). Abelian uniqueness gives one harmonic extension. The two coupled Dirac determinant evaluations are \\(0,\\pm4\\), so index is zero. Positive scalar curvature kills the positive Dirac kernel, and zero index kills the cokernel. The eigenline degree is one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Theorem 8.16", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The only fixed-type tuples meeting nonphase cuts are legitimate unbroken isolated type \\(A\\) instantons, with no losses.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Choose outside delays and exceptional evaluations as below, then use Proposition 10 to obtain \\(\\Omega\\ne0\\), \\(n_D^{\\rm tot}&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set \\(\\eta=n_D^{\\rm tot}-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">A main field is type \\(A\\) if its spinor is zero and its connection irreducible; type \\(R\\) if zero-spinor with continuous stabilizer; type \\(S\\) if a nonzero spinor lies in one parallel line; type \\(P\\) otherwise. A fixed-type tuple has no \\(P\\).</p><p class=\"li\">At a splitting, order the active line first and put\n\\[\nv=c_1(L_1)-c_1(L_2),\\qquad K=\\Lambda+v.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Virtually fill true lens and \\(J\\)-ends. If there are \\(k\\) true \\(J\\)-cuts, there are \\(k+1\\) ordered main components. Let \\(p_i\\) count retained interval parameters with lens parameters restored, \\(s_i\\) assigned sphere tests, \\(z_i\\) cap degree, and\n\\[\nq_i=D_{I,i}+p_i-2s_i-z_i,\\qquad\ni_i=q_i+2n_{D,i}.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the following requirements, originally Definition 8.7:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. Fixed-type \\(A\\)-equations with retained cuts and pure \\(S\\)-tangential equations are transverse; \\(q_i\\ge0\\) on \\(A\\), strictly with positive losses, and \\(d_s+p_i+4\\ell_i\\ge0\\) on \\(S\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. A zero-evaluation assigned sphere event requires a distinct unit of \\(\\ell_i\\), with the whole, switching, and assigned-half conventions of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. For a tuple containing \\(P\\), deleting all \\(R\\)-fields, their particles, and their tests leaves independent retained equations and a regular necessary projection of dimension at most the formula below.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The only fixed-type tuples meeting nonphase cuts are legitimate unbroken isolated type \\(A\\) instantons, with no losses. After the \\(\\eta\\) complex phase cuts and effective phase quotient, the free problem has expected dimension one. Its only possible true-face or positive-loss limits are one separated trace-minimum lens cap and one type \\(P\\) main component, with no other cut, particle, or loss.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose outside data by Lemma 12.4 and then obtain the large finite stack by Proposition 10. Lemma 12.5 proves the fixed-type exclusion. The ordinary dimension condition and \\(\\eta=n_D^{\\rm tot}-1\\) give\n\\[\n(D_I^{\\rm tot}+n-2n-z)+2n_D^{\\rm tot}-2\\eta-1=1.\n\\]\nLemma 12.6 gives the asserted free degenerations. These are numerical conclusions under explicit regularity and independence hypotheses; the following propositions construct those hypotheses.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.1", "orig": "Lemmas 8.6 and 8.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The virtual numbers satisfy \\( \\sum_i(q_i+4)=4,\\qquad \\sum_i i_i-2\\eta=2-4k. \\) For \\(R,S\\), \\( \\kappa_i=-v_i^2/4+\\ell_i,\\qquad \\ell_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}. \\) A particle of weight \\(a\\) restores at most \\(4a\\) dimensions …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The virtual numbers satisfy\n\\[\n\\sum_i(q_i+4)=4,\\qquad\n\\sum_i i_i-2\\eta=2-4k.\n\\]\nFor \\(R,S\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa_i=-v_i^2/4+\\ell_i,\\qquad \\ell_i\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}.\n\\]\nA particle of weight \\(a\\) restores at most \\(4a\\) dimensions through position and dropped cuts; its net penalty is at least four in ASD dimension and two in spinor dimension. A separated lens has penalty \\(\\delta_I-1\\) or \\(\\delta_{\\rm sp}-1\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Across each \\(J\\), filled ASD indices lose three and interval parameters lose one. Dirac indices add without correction. Thus\n\\[\n\\sum D_{I,i}=D_I^{\\rm tot}-3k,\\quad\n\\sum p_i=n-k,\\quad\\sum s_i=n,\\quad\\sum z_i=z,\n\\]\ngiving \\(\\sum q_i=-4k\\). Split reference fillings from Proposition 11 leave nonnegative integral excess.</p><p>The exact support cases are: unconstrained position \\(4+0\\); one surface or sphere hit \\(2+2\\); two transverse cap surfaces \\(0+4\\); fixed point \\(0+4\\). General position excludes all other simultaneous basic incidences. Removing charge \\(a\\) lowers ASD index by \\(8a\\) and spinor index by \\(6a\\). Removing a lens filling lowers field index by \\(\\delta\\), removes one parameter, and omits a degree-two test, giving net penalty \\(\\delta-1\\). Proposition 11 makes every tested lens penalty positive.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.2", "orig": "Lemma 8.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Combine the positive-cell square with the negative half squares selected by Proposition 8’s clamp.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">On a positive cell write \\(x=vG\\), \\(u=vU\\), \\(t_b=vT_b\\), and adjacent negative halves \\(h_1,h_2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Whole evaluations are\n\\[\nd_1=x-\\sum t_b-h_1,\\qquad d_2=x-2u-h_2.\n\\]</p><p class=\"li\">Exactly two \\(t_b\\) are odd; \\(x,h_i,d_i\\) are even.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Combine the positive-cell square with the negative half squares selected by Proposition 8’s clamp. The result is at most \\(-2\\), except possibly for type \\(S\\), a singleton \\(I\\), and \\(u=z_I=\\pm1\\). In that exception the square is at most two and the selected whole evaluation is zero. If \\(u=0\\), the positive-cell square alone is at most \\(-2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For a singleton or pair, completing squares gives\n\\[\nQ_{\\{1\\}}=8z_Iu-4u^2-\\sum(t_b-u)^2-\\tfrac12(h_1-2u)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_{\\{2\\}}=8z_Iu-4u^2-\\sum t_b^2-\\tfrac12(h_2-2u)^2,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ_{\\{1,2\\}}=4z_Iu-2u^2-\\sum(t_b-u/2)^2\n-\\tfrac12\\sum(h_i-u)^2.\n\\]\nFor \\(R\\), \\(\\operatorname{sign}(u)z_I\\le0\\). For \\(S\\), singleton thresholds \\(1/2,3/2\\) and half-integrality give respectively values at most zero or one. The singleton exceptional-square sum is at least two, so\n\\[\nQ\\le8|u|-4u^2-2.\n\\]\nOnly \\(|u|=1\\), \\(\\operatorname{sign}(u)z_I=1\\) can exceed \\(-2\\), and then \\(d_i=4(z_I-u)=0\\). For two sides the threshold gives \\(\\operatorname{sign}(u)z_I\\le1/2\\), while parity makes the remaining square sum at least two; hence \\(Q\\le2|u|-2u^2-2\\le-2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.3", "orig": "Lemmas 8.10–8.11", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For an interior fixed \\(R\\)- or \\(S\\)-component with \\(L_i\\) cells and \\(m_i\\) positive cells, \\( 8\\kappa_i\\ge\\max(4s_i-4m_i,4m_i), \\) \\( q_i+4\\ge\\max(2s_i+L_i-7m_i,\\ m_i+L_i-2s_i). \\) Every nonempty contiguous interior …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For an interior fixed \\(R\\)- or \\(S\\)-component with \\(L_i\\) cells and \\(m_i\\) positive cells,\n\\[\n8\\kappa_i\\ge\\max(4s_i-4m_i,4m_i),\n\\]\n\\[\nq_i+4\\ge\\max(2s_i+L_i-7m_i,\\ m_i+L_i-2s_i).\n\\]\nEvery nonempty contiguous interior \\(R,S\\)-run satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum(q_i+4)\\ge-2.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Reserve two adjacent test slots and the clamp-used negative halves for each positive cell. Let \\(s_P\\le2m_i\\) be occupied positive slots and \\(s_N=s_i-s_P\\). Every other test charges one nonzero even negative half or a distinct loss unit. If \\(z_0\\) negative assignments have no nonzero half and \\(e_0\\) positive cells are exceptional, the incidence requirement gives\n\\[\n\\ell_i\\ge z_0+e_0,\n\\]\n\\[\n8\\kappa_i\\ge4(s_N-z_0)+4m_i-8e_0+8\\ell_i\n\\ge4s_N+4m_i.\n\\]\nNo half or loss unit is charged twice. Since \\(p_i=L_i-1\\), \\(b_i^+=m_i\\),\n\\[\nq_i+4=8\\kappa_i+L_i-3m_i-2s_i.\n\\]</p><p>For a run, write totals \\(L,m,s\\). Then \\(L-1\\le s\\le L+1\\), and spacing gives \\(L\\ge4m-3\\). Summing either inequality yields\n\\[\n\\sum(q_i+4)\\ge\\max(2s+L-7m,\\ m+L-2s).\n\\]\nFor \\(m\\ge2\\) this is at least \\(-1\\); for \\(m=1,L\\ge3\\), at least zero. The short cases \\((m,L)=(1,1),(1,2)\\) have minima \\(-2,-1\\), respectively. For \\(m=0\\), the first bound is positive.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.4", "orig": "Lemma 8.12", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Before choosing middle repetitions, outside delays and then odd extra exceptional evaluations can be chosen so that no outside component is \\(R\\), and every outside \\(S\\)-component satisfying the fixed-type requirements …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Before choosing middle repetitions, outside delays and then odd extra exceptional evaluations can be chosen so that no outside component is \\(R\\), and every outside \\(S\\)-component satisfying the fixed-type requirements obeys\n\\[\nq_i+4\\ge8.\n\\]\nThis includes a component containing both caps.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 8 excludes cap-containing \\(R\\). Its two old-cap square bounds, the incidence accounting, and orthogonal splitting give\n\\[\n8\\kappa_i\\ge4s_i-4m_i+\n2\\sum_{E_{\\rm exc}\\subset i}v(E_{\\rm exc})^2-C_1.\n\\]\nWith \\(p_i=L_i+O(1)\\), \\(s_i\\ge L_i-O(1)\\), and \\(b_i^+=m_i+O(1)\\),\n\\[\nq_i\\ge3L_i-7m_i+\n2\\sum v(E_{\\rm exc})^2-C.\n\\]\nSpacing makes the first two terms grow at least as \\((5L_i-21)/4\\). Choose \\(L_*\\) so long end components already have \\(q_i\\ge4\\), then delay positive cells past \\(L_*\\).</p><p>A shorter offending component has no positive cell. If \\(q_i&lt;4\\), its bounded topology and the \\(v\\)-cap bound give bounded \\(\\kappa_i,\\ell_i\\). Tangential regularity gives\n\\[\nK_i^2\\ge2\\chi_i+3\\sigma_i-4p_i-16\\ell_i\\ge-C_K.\n\\]\nThe \\(K\\)-cap bound and negative remaining summands give\n\\[\nK_i^2\\le C_K'-\\sum K(E_{\\rm exc})^2.\n\\]\nThus \\(|K(E_{\\rm exc})|\\le B\\), independently of the new \\(\\Lambda\\)-values. Since\n\\[\n|v(E_{\\rm exc})|\\ge|\\Lambda(E_{\\rm exc})|-B,\n\\]\nchoose the odd \\(\\Lambda\\)-values sufficiently large to contradict \\(q_i&lt;4\\). Only afterwards choose repetitions and finite metric thresholds.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.5", "orig": "Proposition 8.13 and Lemma 8.14", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A fixed-type tuple satisfying the first two requirements in Proposition 12 is a single legitimate, unbroken, loss-free type \\(A\\) instanton.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A fixed-type tuple satisfying the first two requirements in Proposition 12 is a single legitimate, unbroken, loss-free type \\(A\\) instanton.</p><p>For a nonempty run of \\(r\\) interior \\(R\\)-components,\n\\[\n\\sum(4+i_i-p_i)\\ge-2.\n\\]\nMore precisely, if \\(s,m,s_P\\) are its assigned tests, positive cells, and occupied positive slots, \\(\\mathrm{miss}=2m-s_P\\), and \\(\\Delta\\) its endpoint-bit difference, then\n\\[\n\\sum(4+i_i-p_i)\\ge s-4m+r+\\Delta+3\\,\\mathrm{miss}.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(k&gt;0\\), let \\(a\\) count all \\(A\\)-components and \\(b\\in\\{0,1,2\\}\\) count outside \\(S\\)-components. There are \\(a+b\\ge2\\) distinguished components; the remaining pieces form at most \\(a+b-1\\) interior runs. Hence\n\\[\n\\sum(q_i+4)\\ge4a+8b-2(a+b-1)\\ge6,\n\\]\ncontradicting the sum four. If \\(k=0\\), the one component has \\(q=0\\), cannot be \\(R\\) or \\(S\\), and \\(A\\)-regularity excludes all positive losses.</p><p>For \\(R\\), Lemma 12.2 has no exceptional case. Using\n\\[\ni_i-p_i=6\\kappa_i-3-m_i+\\Delta_i-2s_i\n\\]\nand \\(6\\kappa_i\\ge3s_{N,i}+3m_i\\) gives the sharper displayed inequality. If \\(m=0\\), its right side is nonnegative. Otherwise write\n\\[\nL\\ge4m-3+a_0+b_0,\\qquad\ns=L-1+\\epsilon_a+\\epsilon_b.\n\\]\nEach zero-offset endpoint without an assigned test leaves a distinct missing slot, and\n\\[\na_0+\\epsilon_a+3\\,1_{\\{a_0=\\epsilon_a=0\\}}\\ge1\n\\]\nat each end. Substitution gives at least \\(r+\\Delta-2\\ge-2\\), without borrowing an exterior charge.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 12.6", "orig": "Proposition 8.15", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For a tuple containing \\(P\\), suppose its necessary projection has dimension before phase cuts and quotient at most …", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For a tuple containing \\(P\\), suppose its necessary projection has dimension before phase cuts and quotient at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\notin R}\\left(i_i-\\sum_{\\text{lens ends of }i}\n(\\delta_{\\rm sp}-1)-2w_i\\right)+\\sum_{i\\in R}p_i,\n\\]\nwhere \\(w_i\\) counts main-particle and allocated nonlens integer loss. If this projected problem is regular at candidate projections, the tuple has no \\(J\\)-cut and no integer loss. Its only alternatives are an unbroken free field or one trace-minimum lens end with one \\(P\\)-main component.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(N_{\\neg R}\\) count non-\\(R\\) components. Every lens penalty is at least one. Subtracting \\(2\\eta+1\\), the dimension is at most\n\\[\n5-4N_{\\neg R}\n-\\sum_{i\\in R}(4+i_i-p_i)\n-\\sum_{i\\notin R}(N_{{\\rm lens},i}+2w_i).\n\\]\nIf \\(k&gt;0\\), both outside components are non-\\(R\\), so \\(N_{\\neg R}\\ge2\\), and there are at most \\(N_{\\neg R}-1\\) \\(R\\)-runs. Lemma 12.5 bounds the dimension by \\(3-2N_{\\neg R}\\le-1\\). Hence \\(k=0\\).</p><p>There is then one \\(P\\)-main component and dimension at most \\(1-N_{\\rm lens}-2w\\). Thus \\(w=0\\), \\(N_{\\rm lens}\\le1\\). The actual central lens penalty is at least five, while trace charge \\(1/4+j\\) has penalty \\(1+6j\\). Only one trace end of charge \\(1/4\\) survives. Proposition 11 excludes particles and nonconstant levels inside it.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Sections 9.1–9.6; Lemmas 9.4, 9.6–9.11", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The required equation and cut variations, exact support rules, body and neck compactness, and finite candidate degeneration framework exist with arbitrarily small prescribed norms.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix one finite stack and the permitted metric and perturbation tolerances. Only true \\(J\\)- and lens lengths may diverge.</p><p class=\"li\">Use little-Hölder field spaces \\(c^{a,\\alpha}_\\delta\\), \\(a\\ge3\\), and equation spaces of one lower order, with \\(0&lt;\\alpha&lt;1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Gauges have independent parallel limiting constants and decaying remainders.</p><p class=\"li\">An ideal candidate retains completed main fields, weighted main particles, retained cuts and marks, and nonnegative discrete lens and connector charges, but omits actual cap and connector fields and all matching conditions.</p><p class=\"li\">Perturbations are component-local at true faces; mixed terms use independent frames and common phase, and are supported in finite-isotropy sample tubes. Separated lens caps have no mixed coupling or Dirac forcing.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The required equation and cut variations, exact support rules, body and neck compactness, and finite candidate degeneration framework exist with arbitrarily small prescribed norms. After deleting \\(R\\)-fields, every retained mixed equation and cut is independent of their fields, positions, and tests. These constructions preserve the neighborhood on which the ordinary integer is \\(\\Omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the weighted countable sampling library with every required fixed-order and exponential-tail seminorm dominated by its coefficient norm. Terms and cutoff balls remain component-local, and mixed finite lists have total weight one. Lemmas 13.1–13.2 establish the available universal variations and differentiability. Lemmas 13.3–13.5 prove compactness and exact incidence. Lemma 13.6 supplies the finite degeneration framework for later projected induction.</p><p>All additions are made in a small relative open ball of the ordinary genuine-face-preserving data from Proposition 10. The coefficient norm controls the finitely many norms defining that neighborhood, so \\(\\Omega\\) is preserved.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.1", "orig": "Lemmas 9.4 and 9.6", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Finite framed samples avoiding any prescribed finite particle set detect stabilizers.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Finite framed samples avoiding any prescribed finite particle set detect stabilizers. They supply arbitrary adjoint equation and cut values at \\(A\\), arbitrary pure tangential values at \\(S\\), and, with a \\(P\\)-witness, arbitrary full values at every non-\\(R\\) target using finite rational branch lists. Phase-section values may be varied using the witness alone.</p><p>Mixed terms can vanish on every fixed-type tuple. At exact split faces they vanish near samples involving an \\(R\\)-field, so deletion of \\(R\\)-data leaves genuinely independent retained formulas.</p>", "proof": "<p>A parallel gauge is determined by its base value commuting with holonomies and fixing transported spinor samples, with common phase included. One noncentral holonomy reduces the centralizer to a circle, two noncommuting holonomies to the center; finitely many vector conditions detect the remaining stabilizer. Paths can avoid finitely many particles.</p><p>At \\(A\\), the center acts trivially on adjoint outputs. At \\(S\\), invariant outputs are exactly diagonal curvature and active-line spinors. Extend these values over compact sample slice tubes.</p><p>A \\(P\\)-field cannot compensate a phase outside \\(\\{\\pm1\\}\\), since the compensating parallel automorphism would produce a splitting and active line. Independent target and witness frames therefore give finite isotropy \\(H\\). In \\(G\\times_HD\\), include all \\(H\\)-translates of a slice output, each of weight \\(1/|H|\\), with coefficients containing the entire \\(H\\)-span. Each branch has full coefficient range. Cut off inside the tube and extend by repeated zero branches.</p><p>A fixed-type tuple has continuous compensating phase isotropy; an \\(R\\)-sample has continuous independent frame isotropy. Both avoid the closed support of a mixed finite-isotropy term. Every cutoff ball stays on its graph’s component, so retained atomic cutoff formulas also omit discarded positions.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.2", "orig": "Lemma 9.7, with the universal regularity argument of Section 9.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Sampling equations are \\(C^1\\) from \\(c^{a+s,\\alpha}_\\delta\\) to one-lower-order targets, and \\(C^j\\) into a fixed target from sufficiently high field order.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Sampling equations are \\(C^1\\) from \\(c^{a+s,\\alpha}_\\delta\\) to one-lower-order targets, and \\(C^j\\) into a fixed target from sufficiently high field order. At a smooth universally surjective zero, compatible implicit charts have every prescribed finite differentiability order, with the same perturbation space and smooth kernel coordinates. Available values from Lemma 13.1 give universal full or tangential surjectivity in the stated non-\\(R\\) models.</p>", "proof": "<p>Moving evaluation or transport uses one field derivative; little-Hölder translation is continuous. Graph derivatives are order zero, and energy-cutoff derivatives are scalar functionals multiplying smooth outputs. Weighted summability preserves all fixed-order bounds and compactness of derivatives.</p><p>Choose finitely many smooth perturbation directions spanning the lowest-order cokernel and common complements to the smooth kernel. The \\(C^1\\) implicit graphs at higher levels agree by uniqueness. Differentiating\n\\[\nB_s(z)D^rv(z)=R_r(z)\n\\]\nby difference quotients at a sufficiently high level, then applying the fixed lower-level inverse, proves \\(C^{r+1}\\) regularity without repeatedly differentiating a same-order inverse.</p><p>For surjectivity first use \\(W^{1,p}_{\\delta_S}\\to L^p_{\\delta_S}\\), \\(p&gt;4\\), \\(\\delta_S&lt;\\delta_H\\). Gauge-row cokernels have asymptotes \\(a_e+tb_e+O(e^{-\\epsilon t})\\). Pairing with independently allowed cutoff parallel constants forces every \\(b_e=0\\); integration then makes the cokernel a global infinitesimal stabilizer, zero on non-\\(R\\). At \\(R\\), the gauge target is the annihilator of global stabilizers. Smooth compact equation outputs and independent cut values kill the remaining cokernel. Primal bootstrap and root-free spectral estimates transfer to weight \\(\\delta_H\\) and higher Hölder order. Apply Sard–Smale on finite branch lifts.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.3", "orig": "Lemma 9.8", "depth": 1, "snippet": "For bounded permitted data, including coefficient-convergent ideal sequences:", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For bounded permitted data, including coefficient-convergent ideal sequences:\n\\[\n\\|F_a^0\\|_2^2+\\|\\nabla_a\\Phi\\|_2^2+\\|\\Phi\\|_4^4\\le C.\n\\]\nNeither \\(|\\Phi|^4\\) nor \\(|F_a^{0,+}|^2\\) has atoms. A subsequence converges modulo gauge away from finitely many removable points; positive integral charge \\(m\\) is lost there with mass \\(8\\pi^2m\\). The limit solves the atom-dependent cutoff equations. With no further loss, convergence holds in completed higher-order charts; at a new particle it still holds in \\(C^{0,\\beta}\\), \\(\\beta&lt;1\\), on surviving exact cut paths.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 8.2, the bounded negative zeroth-order costs, and Chern–Weil give the energy estimate. For a cutoff on \\(B_{2r}\\), equal to one on \\(B_r\\),\n\\[\nc\\int_{B_r}|\\Phi|^4\n\\le Cr^{-2}\\int_{B_{2r}\\setminus B_r}|\\Phi|^2+o_r(1)\n\\le C\\left(\\int_{B_{2r}\\setminus B_r}|\\Phi|^4\\right)^{1/2}+o_r(1).\n\\]\nPassing to weak measures and then shrinking \\(r\\) kills atoms. The curvature equation gives the same for self-dual curvature.</p><p>Off concentration points, small-energy Coulomb gauge gives small \\(L^4\\) connection coefficients. The first-order system has form \\(\\mathcal Du=C(u)u+b\\), \\(|C(u)|\\le C|u|\\), \\(b\\) bounded. Absorb the small critical \\(L^4\\) potential in \\(W^{1,p}\\), \\(2&lt;p&lt;4\\), then choose \\(p&gt;8/3\\) and bootstrap to \\(C^{0,\\beta}\\). Active cutoff graphs acquire higher regularity on nested balls. Thickened ordinary \\(Y\\)-holonomy terms converge by almost-everywhere transport convergence and dominated convergence; no uniform high derivatives through a new particle are asserted.</p><p>Graphs meeting atoms are disabled; all other cutoff limits are exactly\n\\[\n\\int\\chi(|F_a^0|^2+|\\Phi|^4)+8\\pi^2\\sum m_j\\chi(x_j).\n\\]\nAnnular small-energy gauges and cutoffs give a \\(W^{1,2}\\) removable extension, then elliptic bootstrap. Comparing Chern integrals on a strongly convergent surrounding sphere identifies the loss with an integer clutching degree. Nonconcentration of self-dual curvature makes positive mass equal to \\(8\\pi^2m\\). Colliding ideal labels add their weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.4", "orig": "Lemma 9.9", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A sequence with diverging true neck lengths has finitely many particles and nonconstant cylinder levels.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A sequence with diverging true neck lengths has finitely many particles and nonconstant cylinder levels. Every translated level has zero spinor and is ASD, with discrete positive nonconstant charge cost. Remaining no-loss stretches converge exponentially to matching flat orbits and carry no diffuse loss.</p>", "proof": "<p>Deep translation removes background and graph tails. Positive scalar curvature and finite-energy Weitzenböck cutoffs kill the limiting spinor. The finite flat lists on \\(S^3\\) and lens spaces have \\(H^1_{\\rm ad}=0\\) and invertible flat Dirac operators.</p><p>Near a flat \\(\\gamma\\), remove temporal parallel modes and use the spatial Coulomb slice. Then\n\\[\n(\\partial_t+B_\\gamma)u=N(u)+f,\\qquad\n\\|N(u)\\|_2\\le C\\epsilon\\|u\\|_2.\n\\]\nPropagate the gapped positive and negative spectral modes from opposite ends in \\(L^2\\). On unit strips the resulting convolution estimate, followed by local elliptic estimates and absorption, gives\n\\[\nA_j\\le C_k(e^{-\\delta j}A_-+e^{-\\delta(M-1-j)}A_+)\n+C_k\\sum_re^{-\\delta|j-r|}F_r,\n\\]\nuniformly in \\(M\\). This uses the nonsingular \\(L^2\\) semigroup bound before gaining derivatives.</p><p>Finite-energy tails remain near one isolated flat and converge exponentially. Only then use Chern–Simons discreteness to bound the number of positive-charge levels. Any remaining bad strip would extract another level or particle. On the residual slice, same-flat Chern–Simons differences vanish; the spinor and self-dual estimates plus Chern–Weil exclude diffuse charge. Parallel end rotations are allowed and create no forcing.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.5", "orig": "Lemma 9.10", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A basic sphere, surface, or point test persists unless a particle meets its exact basic support.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A basic sphere, surface, or point test persists unless a particle meets its exact basic support. Dropping such a test and its mark, while retaining the incidence, restores at most four dimensions per particle weight. The zero-evaluation sphere rules in Proposition 12 hold with distinct loss units.</p>", "proof": "<p>On an unhit sphere support, \\(C^{0,\\beta}\\)-convergence with \\(\\beta&gt;b\\) gives \\(H^b\\)-convergence of paths, hence persistence of the zero-winding modification. Sample corrections hitting atoms vanish, without invalidating the limiting formula. At \\(J\\), identity segments cannot contain event limits and surviving events are intrinsic to one compact half support. Cap sections have the same property.</p><p>Retain a preimage in the two-dimensional sphere or surface domain for an incidence; immersion is unnecessary. The support-position budget is exactly that of Lemma 12.1. Finite switching charts and their limiting unions preserve closed incidence.</p><p>For a parallel field, zero whole evaluation, zero switching halves, or zero assigned-half evaluation gives uniform avoidance unless its basic support is hit. At a tested lens, any zero-reference alternative requires the allowed excess charge. Disjoint basic supports prevent one particle unit from paying for two tests. Moving a particle into a cap transfers its integer weight to that cap; escaping unconstrained connector particles create no new tested incidence.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 13.6", "orig": "Lemma 9.11", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Candidate degeneration data have finite ranges.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Candidate degeneration data have finite ranges. Order them by deeper true faces; larger total effective end and particle loss; fewer main locations at fixed loss; more dropped tests; and more \\(R\\)-components, treating finite assignments, support strata, and rank choices together. Every coefficient-convergent candidate sequence either converges at the same step, limits to an earlier step, or becomes fixed type.</p>", "proof": "<p>The completed main equations first bound spinor and self-dual energy independently of charge. If \\(\\kappa_i^{\\rm main}\\) excludes omitted fields and particles, then\n\\[\n\\kappa_i^{\\rm main}\\ge-\\frac{B_i}{4\\pi^2},\\qquad\n\\mathcal L\\le\\kappa^{\\rm tot}+\\sum_i\\frac{B_i}{4\\pi^2},\n\\]\n\\[\n\\kappa_i^{\\rm main}\\le\\kappa^{\\rm tot}\n+\\sum_{j\\ne i}\\frac{B_j}{4\\pi^2}.\n\\]\nThus full main curvature and all nonnegative discrete losses are bounded. Relative \\(c_2\\)-sectors are finite; compact dual forms bound every coordinate of split integral classes, with finitely many torsion choices.</p><p>Apply Lemmas 13.3–13.4. New cuts or losses move earlier. If a lens main asymptote changes through an extracted level of charge \\(e&gt;0\\), replace its assignment by \\(\\kappa_N'=\\kappa_N+e\\); congruences and the tested positive range persist without reconstructing an omitted cap. Escaping labelled particles or collisions reduce main locations. Exact support hits add dropped tests, and compact intrinsic half supports prevent incident particles from escaping down an already separated \\(J\\). \\(R\\) is closed under completed convergence. With no worsening, no loss remains and higher-order completed convergence holds. Full matrix neighborhoods include rank-zero limits of rank-one cuts.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 14", "orig": "Propositions 9.12–9.13", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Arbitrarily small single-valued, single-component data establish the first two requirements of Proposition 12.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed finite problem, \\(\\Omega\\ne0\\), and \\(r=n_D^{\\rm tot}&gt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\eta=r-1\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Arbitrarily small single-valued, single-component data establish the first two requirements of Proposition 12. The fixed-type locus is a finite set of legitimate unbroken cut-down instantons, with surjective complex coupled Dirac operator at each.</p><p>Near each such instanton \\(a\\), the normal spinor link is \\(S^{2r-1}/\\{\\pm1\\}\\), its phase quotient is \\(\\mathbb{CP}^{r-1}\\), and the original-phase-character-two line is \\(\\mathcal O(2)\\). The \\(\\eta\\) phase cuts have regular link count\n\\[\n2^\\eta\\operatorname{sign}(a),\n\\]\nup to one common boundary-orientation convention. Later perturbations may vanish near these collars.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemmas 14.1–14.2 establish the fixed stage and its stable finite instanton set. Install the phase sections only after these choices are final. Lemma 14.3 gives their local collars and the doubled-hyperplane degree. Later mixed and interior choices are required to vanish on the protected neighborhoods, so neither centers nor leading quadratic sections move.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.1", "orig": "Proposition 9.12, fixed-type stage", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The required \\(A\\)-equations with retained tests and pure \\(S\\)-tangential equations can be made simultaneously transverse on all relevant ideal, parameter, position, and rank strata.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The required \\(A\\)-equations with retained tests and pure \\(S\\)-tangential equations can be made simultaneously transverse on all relevant ideal, parameter, position, and rank strata. Their nonempty fixed-type tuples are precisely the legitimate unbroken instantons, forming a finite set.</p>", "proof": "<p>Apply Proposition 13’s single-component universal variations and Sard–Smale to countably many strata inside the permitted small ball. At true faces use only retained component parameters.</p><p>On \\(A\\), virtual index is \\(q_i\\); every tested lens has positive penalty \\(\\delta_I-1\\), every particle at least four, and every allocated positive connector loss positive penalty. Thus nonempty regular problems have \\(q_i\\ge0\\), strictly with loss. On \\(S\\), use Proposition 11’s zero-correction sharp tangential references, ignore tests, and allow at most \\(4\\ell_i\\) position parameters, giving \\(d_s+p_i+4\\ell_i\\ge0\\). Exact incidence is Proposition 13.</p><p>Proposition 12’s fixed-type argument now applies. Compactness makes the resulting regular zero-dimensional instanton set compact and hence finite.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.2", "orig": "Proposition 9.12, Dirac regularization", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The complex Dirac operator at each legitimate instanton can be made surjective without changing its zero-spinor equations or ordinary cuts, while preserving all fixed-type exclusions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The complex Dirac operator at each legitimate instanton can be made surjective without changing its zero-spinor equations or ordinary cuts, while preserving all fixed-type exclusions.</p>", "proof": "<p>Phase equivariance forces the Dirac perturbation value at zero spinor to vanish, but allows arbitrary complex-linear compact spinor derivative. Finite transported samples inject the Dirac kernel, and localized outputs detect its cokernel, giving all kernel-to-cokernel maps. Since the complex index is \\(r&gt;0\\), generic small such maps are surjective.</p><p>Treat finitely many instantons in disjoint configuration neighborhoods. These conditions are open. Reselect generic first-stage data within their open surjectivity neighborhoods. A newly appearing forbidden sequence has an excluded ideal limit; an unbroken reduction cannot limit to an irreducible instanton because its parallel splitting persists. Regular isolated ASD neighborhoods contain all nearby instantons. Thus the exclusions and ordinary count remain intact.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 14.3", "orig": "Proposition 9.13", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The local link and its phase-cut count are those asserted in Proposition 14.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The local link and its phase-cut count are those asserted in Proposition 14.</p>", "proof": "<p>The regular isolated cut-down ASD problem and surjective Dirac operator allow the implicit theorem to solve transverse coordinates in terms of the complex kernel \\(K\\cong\\mathbb C^r\\). The center identifies \\(\\psi\\) and \\(-\\psi\\). Writing \\(\\Phi=\\rho\\psi\\), \\(\\|\\psi\\|=1\\), dividing the Dirac equation by \\(\\rho\\), and radially blowing up gives boundary \\(S(K)/\\{\\pm1\\}\\), with phase quotient \\(\\mathbb P(K)\\). Its orientation is the complex orientation times the ordinary instanton sign.</p><p>Choose phase sections quadratic to leading order using finite spinor samples. Dividing by \\(\\rho^2\\) gives homogeneous quadratic forms, hence \\(\\mathcal O(2)\\), not \\(\\mathcal O(1)\\): the effective circle bundle has doubled Hopf transitions. Explicitly choose\n\\[\nz_j^2-a_jz_0^2,\\qquad a_j\\ne0,\\quad1\\le j\\le r-1.\n\\]\nTheir \\(2^{r-1}\\) common zeros have invertible complex Jacobian and positive complex signs. For \\(r=1\\) there is one point and no cut. The implicit theorem gives regular collars. Protect smaller collars from all later variations; repeated zero branches there have total weight one.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 15", "orig": "Lemma 9.14 and Proposition 9.15", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Finitely many additional local rational multisection terms, with arbitrarily small coefficients, exclude every forbidden mixed candidate.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Start with Proposition 14’s ultimate first-stage data.</p><p class=\"li\">Candidate tuples retain all main equations and charge assignments as in Proposition 13.</p><p class=\"li\">In a tuple containing \\(P\\), discard \\(R\\)-fields, their positions, and their tests, but retain their parameter variables.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Finitely many additional local rational multisection terms, with arbitrarily small coefficients, exclude every forbidden mixed candidate. They establish the third requirement of Proposition 12 only near candidate projections. The remaining types are the unbroken free part and the one trace-minimum lens main problem, the latter regular of dimension zero. Choices agree on paired lens exteriors and vanish near protected instanton collars.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 15.1 supplies the necessary projection and its local variations. Lemma 15.2 converts every negative projected dimension into absence without asserting compactness for unrelated zeros of the loosened projection. Lemma 15.3 regularizes the only remaining true-face main problem. Unbroken free interior regularization is deferred until its end collars are constructed.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.14", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Near candidate projections, deletion defines a Fredholm necessary problem with finite isotropy.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Near candidate projections, deletion defines a Fredholm necessary problem with finite isotropy. Before phase quotient and phase cuts, its dimension is at most\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\notin R}\n\\left(i_i-\\sum_{\\text{lens ends of }i}(\\delta_{\\rm sp}-1)-2w_i\\right)\n+\\sum_{i\\in R}p_i.\n\\]\nPhase cuts and quotient subtract \\(2\\eta+1\\). Local finite-branch variations make its universal equations and retained cuts transverse.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 13 gives independence of every retained formula, including atomic cutoff terms. A \\(P\\)-witness gives finite isotropy. Parameterize dropped-test incidences by preimages in the sphere or surface domains, or transverse fiber products for two surfaces.</p><p>Virtual field index is \\(i_i\\). Removing a lens filling costs \\(\\delta_{\\rm sp}-1\\); each integer main-particle or allocated connector loss costs at least two after all permitted position and incidence recovery. Removed \\(R\\)-components contribute only \\(p_i\\). Rank-zero strata can only decrease dimension. The common phase has finite stabilizer. Full target values, full cut matrices, holonomy-event values, and phase-section values are available by Proposition 13, yielding universal surjectivity on each branch.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.2", "orig": "Proposition 9.15, finite exclusion", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every forbidden mixed candidate type can be eliminated by a finite sequence of arbitrarily small finite-branch additions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every forbidden mixed candidate type can be eliminated by a finite sequence of arbitrarily small finite-branch additions. Previously completed exclusions remain absent.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 12 computes strictly negative necessary projected index for each forbidden type before any regularity conclusion. Use the finite ordering of Proposition 13.</p><p>At a current step, retain \\(R\\)-fields before projecting. Its candidate image is compact modulo slices and finite assignments: a sequence limits at the same step, an already excluded earlier step, or an excluded fixed tuple. A forbidden candidate cannot approach a clean instanton because existing cuts and positive losses persist; it cannot become the allowed minimum trace end because nondecreasing end loss and an escaping integer particle cannot produce only charge \\(1/4\\).</p><p>Cover the compact image by finitely many sample neighborhoods supplying universal directions. Include all previous branch choices and translates in the coefficient spaces. Surjectivity persists near the compact image. Every candidate for sufficiently small new coefficients projects there; otherwise compactness gives a contradiction. Finite-dimensional parametric transversality, on every branch and rank stratum, makes the negative-index zero sets empty. Choose coefficients small enough to preserve earlier steps and protected collars. There are finitely many steps, each adding finitely many branches.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 15.3", "orig": "Proposition 9.15, trace-main regularity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The remaining trace-minimum main problem is regular zero-dimensional, with identical exterior data under the lens flip.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The remaining trace-minimum main problem is regular zero-dimensional, with identical exterior data under the lens flip.</p>", "proof": "<p>After forbidden candidates are excluded, its candidate set is compact and isolated from forbidden corners. It has one \\(P\\)-main component, so ordinary single-valued variations provide universal surjectivity. Make the generic choices simultaneously on its two exactly identified exteriors and extend them with the prescribed face and bit rules.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 16", "orig": "Propositions 10.1 and 10.4; Lemmas 10.2–10.3", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Arbitrarily small tensor-paired cap choices give finite regular harmonic reducer crossings of signed sum \\(\\pm1\\), zero spinor, and invertible coupled Dirac operator.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Fix the finitely many regular trace-main limits from Proposition 15.</p><p class=\"li\">Frame the minimal cap at a fixed trace-zero representative \\(\\gamma\\), with stabilizer \\(H\\cong U(1)\\), and leave the main component unframed.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix effective phase on the main component by a nonvanishing character-two sample.</p><p class=\"li\">Include all remaining parameters, marks, cuts, and phase-fixing variables in constrained operators; hold lens-neck length \\(D\\) fixed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Arbitrarily small tensor-paired cap choices give finite regular harmonic reducer crossings of signed sum \\(\\pm1\\), zero spinor, and invertible coupled Dirac operator. Each matching pair gives exactly one nearby unbroken zero for every sufficiently large finite \\(D\\), branch by branch, modulo gauge and effective phase. These charts exhaust all converging zeros, preserve orientations and finite branch weights, and introduce no stabilizer or ordering multiplicity.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 16.1 constructs the regular tested caps. Lemmas 16.2–16.3 account for the scalar obstruction and shared parameters. Lemma 16.4 constructs finite-length zeros, and Lemma 16.5 proves exhaustive collars with the required multiplicities. These are zero-particle gluing statements only; no excluded positive-particle stratum is glued.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.1", "orig": "Proposition 10.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The framed charge-\\(1/4\\) cap, with sweep mark and \\(-1\\)-test, is a finite regular set of harmonic crossings fixed by \\(H\\), of signed total absolute value one.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The framed charge-\\(1/4\\) cap, with sweep mark and \\(-1\\)-test, is a finite regular set of harmonic crossings fixed by \\(H\\), of signed total absolute value one. Its spinor is zero and Dirac operator invertible.</p>", "proof": "<p>Proposition 11 gives the unique harmonic extension, framed tangential index zero, complex normal ASD index one, and invertible complex Dirac operator. Based holonomy derivatives detect its normal kernel modulo framed gauge: a variation producing only simultaneous conjugations defines a gauge by path transport, whose off-diagonal part decays because \\(\\gamma\\) has no such parallel section.</p><p>Localized equivariant outputs vanishing on commuting data realize every complex kernel-to-cokernel map. A small generic map makes the normal operator surjective, leaving a complex one-dimensional kernel. Make the toral sweep crossings transverse and the normal cut derivative nonzero at each. The combined kernel-and-mark map\n\\[\n\\ker T\\oplus\\mathbb R\\longrightarrow\\mathbb C_{\\rm off}\\oplus\\mathbb R_{\\rm tor}\n\\]\nis an isomorphism. The complex sign is positive, leaving the winding sign; \\(2+1-3=0\\).</p><p>Regular irreducible cap points would have positive-dimensional \\(H\\)-orbits in a zero-dimensional framed set and are absent. Minimum charge excludes particles and tested nonconstant cylinder degenerations. Compactness allows finitely many perturbation neighborhoods, copied to the flipped cap and kept away from protected instantons.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.2", "orig": "Lemma 10.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The positive-weight, phase-fixed, unframed main augmented operator \\(L_M^+\\) is an isomorphism.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The positive-weight, phase-fixed, unframed main augmented operator \\(L_M^+\\) is an isomorphism. The positive-weight framed cap operator \\(L_N^+\\), with full scalar gauge target, has zero kernel and one-dimensional cokernel\n\\[\n\\ell(f)=\\int_N\\langle\\tau,f_{\\rm gauge}\\rangle,\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\tau\\) is the parallel cap generator. Changing the cap weight to \\(-\\delta\\) gives an isomorphism \\(L_N^-\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On the main component, independently allowed limiting gauge constants are essential. For \\(G\\) the gauge action,\n\\[\n\\langle G^*G\\xi,\\xi\\rangle\n=\\|d_a\\xi\\|_2^2+\\|\\xi\\Phi\\|_2^2.\n\\]\nThe kernel vanishes. Dual asymptotes have constant and linear parallel terms; pairing against cutoff constants kills the linear flux, and integration kills the remaining bounded dual solution. Thus the extended gauge inverse and regular isolated phase-fixed main problem give \\(L_M^+\\).</p><p>On the cap, integration against \\(\\tau\\) gives \\(\\ell(L_N^+v)=0\\). The decaying gauge Laplacian has zero kernel and cokernel exactly the global parallel line: one-ended flux kills a linear dual term, then integration forces parallelness. Framed constrained regularity realizes arbitrary nongauge targets, and the gauge inverse corrects precisely the integral-zero scalar row. Hence \\(L_N^+\\) has index \\(-1\\) and zero kernel.</p><p>Changing weight crosses only the one-dimensional temporal zero mode, so \\(L_N^-\\) has index zero. A kernel element has \\(a=c\\tau\\,ds+O(e^{-\\epsilon s})\\). Integrating its gauge equation against \\(\\tau\\) forces \\(c=0\\), reducing it to the positive-weight kernel. Thus it is an isomorphism.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.3", "orig": "Lemma 10.3", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Shared main-parameter derivatives in the cap can be removed by a triangular change of variables, reducing the limiting derivative to \\(L_M^+\\oplus L_N^-\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Shared main-parameter derivatives in the cap can be removed by a triangular change of variables, reducing the limiting derivative to \\(L_M^+\\oplus L_N^-\\). Its cap field correction decays exponentially.</p>", "proof": "<p>Keep the representative, collar gauges, and link fixed. If \\(B_Np\\) is the cap derivative in shared variables, its gauge component is zero and the rest decays; hence \\(\\ell(B_Np)=0\\). Set\n\\[\nKp=-(L_N^+)_{\\ker\\ell}^{-1}B_Np.\n\\]\nSubstitution \\(v_N=w_N+Kp\\) removes the shared cap derivative without changing the main row. Elliptic regularity and the fixed gap give exponential field decay, uniformly over the finite matching list.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.4", "orig": "Proposition 10.4, inverse and nonlinear construction", "depth": 1, "snippet": "At each matching pair the finite-length constrained derivative is invertible for large \\(D\\), with original-norm inverse bounded by \\(Ce^{a\\delta D}\\).", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>At each matching pair the finite-length constrained derivative is invertible for large \\(D\\), with original-norm inverse bounded by \\(Ce^{a\\delta D}\\). Exponentially accurate pregluing then gives a unique zero in an exponentially shrinking neighborhood, smoothly in finite-order solution charts.</p>", "proof": "<p>Preglue through \\(\\gamma\\), giving residual \\(\\|\\mathcal F_D(0)\\|\\le Ce^{-bD}\\). Use the asymmetric neck weight \\(w_D(t)=e^{\\delta t}\\), continued as one on the main core and \\(e^{\\delta D}\\) on the cap core; scale cap marks and cut targets likewise. The main sees \\(+\\delta\\), the cap sees \\(-\\delta\\).</p><p>Transplant Lemma 16.3’s triangular correction. On the weighted cylinder the model is \\(\\partial_t+B-\\delta\\), invertible because \\(\\delta\\notin\\operatorname{spec}B\\), including the temporal zero mode. Patch the two completion estimates and this cylinder inverse with large fixed-width cutoffs. Absorb commutators, coefficient errors, and exponentially controlled graph tails to obtain\n\\[\n\\|v\\|_{X_{D,\\delta}}\\le C_\\delta\\|\\widetilde L_Dv\\|_{Y_{D,\\delta}}.\n\\]\nFixed-length index is zero, hence the derivative is invertible.</p><p>For the nonlinear remainder,\n\\[\n\\|R_D(v)-R_D(v')\\|\\le Ce^{a'\\delta D}r\\|v-v'\\|\n\\]\nin a radius-\\(r\\) ball. Moving samples obey one-derivative-loss estimates; the derivative difference from the smooth preglued base is \\(O(r)\\), using one extra derivative only on that base. Choose \\((2a+a')\\delta&lt;b/2\\). The map\n\\[\nv\\mapsto-Q_D(\\mathcal F_D(0)+R_D(v))\n\\]\ncontracts a ball of radius \\(2C^2e^{-(b-a\\delta)D}\\). The parameterized implicit theorem gives dependence on \\(D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 16.5", "orig": "Proposition 10.4, exhaustion and multiplicity", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Every unbroken zero converging to a specified matching pair lies in its gluing collar for all sufficiently large lengths.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every unbroken zero converging to a specified matching pair lies in its gluing collar for all sufficiently large lengths. The gluing preserves local branch multiplicities and rational weights and gives one match per cap crossing.</p>", "proof": "<p>No loss remains on the lens neck. Proposition 13’s exponential estimate permits flattening both halves with exponentially small residuals. Initially they are \\(o(1)\\)-close in weak positive weights. Put them in fixed completed slices; the cap scalar error has integral zero. The fixed main inverse improves closeness to exponential order. The injective cap estimate, after subtracting \\(Kp\\), does the same.</p><p>The half gauges differ by \\(H\\), absorbed by the reducer. Global gauge slicing costs only polynomially in \\(D\\): a main-core spinor ball controls gauge values, and tube Poincaré gives\n\\[\n\\|\\xi\\|_2^2\\le C(1+D)^2\n\\bigl(\\|d_a\\xi\\|_2^2+\\|\\xi\\Phi\\|_2^2\\bigr).\n\\]\nThus exact zeros enter an exponentially shrinking uniqueness ball from Lemma 16.4.</p><p>The cap is fixed by \\(H\\), so diagonal matching rotations give one point, not a volume or extra factor. It has no spinor phase. Each finite branch list limits to the same matching branch formulas; incompatible crossing terms vanish or are exponentially small. The contraction is equivariant under permutations of local lifts and preserves repeated branch multiplicities and weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 17", "orig": "Theorem 9.1; Lemma 9.16; Proposition 10.5; the boundary formula of Theorem 10.6", "depth": 0, "snippet": "The remaining free stratum admits compatible interior regularization to a compact oriented rationally weighted one-dimensional count after adding its prescribed collars.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Use the finite stack and locally weighted determinant sum from Proposition 10.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(r=n_D^{\\rm tot}&gt;0\\), \\(\\eta=r-1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Use the regularization and protected instanton collars of Proposition 14, the mixed exclusions of Proposition 15, and the exhaustive lens collars of Proposition 16.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The remaining free stratum admits compatible interior regularization to a compact oriented rationally weighted one-dimensional count after adding its prescribed collars. Its instanton-link boundary contribution is\n\\[\n\\sigma_0\\,2^\\eta\\Omega\n\\]\nfor one common \\(\\sigma_0\\in\\{\\pm1\\}\\). Every lens boundary contribution cancels with its bit-flipped partner using exactly the ordinary weights. There are no other boundary types. Consequently\n\\[\n0=\\sigma_0\\,2^\\eta\\Omega.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The ordinary dimension condition and phase cuts give\n\\[\nD_I^{\\rm tot}+2r+n-2n-z-1-2\\eta=1.\n\\]\nProposition 14 identifies every instanton link with \\(\\mathbb{CP}^{r-1}\\), and\n\\[\n\\left\\langle(2h)^{r-1},[\\mathbb{CP}^{r-1}]\\right\\rangle\n=2^{r-1}=2^\\eta.\n\\]\nTheir total weighted boundary is therefore \\(\\sigma_0\\,2^\\eta\\Omega\\).</p><p>Remove small radial instanton neighborhoods and truncate all regular lens collars at large finite length. Lemma 17.3 and the exhaustive collars make the remainder compact. Lemma 17.1 gives zero total oriented weighted boundary. Lemma 17.2 cancels every lens pair with the already fixed ordinary weights. No other end survives. Hence\n\\[\n0=\\sigma_0\\,2^\\eta\\Omega.\n\\]</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.1", "orig": "Lemma 9.16", "depth": 1, "snippet": "A compact oriented one-dimensional zero set formed from the finite compatible branch lists has total weighted oriented boundary zero.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>A compact oriented one-dimensional zero set formed from the finite compatible branch lists has total weighted oriented boundary zero. Weights are rational, and a region where additional branch terms vanish has the original single-valued weight.</p>", "proof": "<p>The surviving unbroken stratum has one \\(P\\)-component. Quotienting the ineffective center makes its gauge-and-phase action free, so local base charts are manifold charts. In sample tubes the translates have weight \\(1/|H|\\); changes of slice permute them with multiplicity.</p><p>At the central candidate, the witness restricts phase to \\(\\{\\pm1\\}\\). Nonzero-spinor targets then have scalar parallel gauges \\(\\pm1\\), and \\(A\\)-targets already have central stabilizers. These act trivially on real adjoint, parameter, and cut directions and complex-linearly on spinor blocks. They preserve determinant orientations. Cutoffs vanish near tube boundaries, where repeated zero branches create no artificial boundary.</p><p>Products have product weights summing to one. A partition of unity on a finite compact cover and ordinary one-dimensional Stokes on each lift cancels overlap derivatives because weighted oriented germs agree. Only weighted boundary points remain.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.2", "orig": "Proposition 10.5", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The two regular minimum trace caps at any lens face, with the identical main branch and exterior data, have opposite oriented contributions after multiplication by the ordinary lift weights.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The two regular minimum trace caps at any lens face, with the identical main branch and exterior data, have opposite oriented contributions after multiplication by the ordinary lift weights. The comparison is independent of the main solution, natural under remaining gluings, and preserves branch weights.</p>", "proof": "<p>The cap tensor operation of Lemma 4.2 changes \\(c\\) and \\(\\Lambda\\) by \\(\\operatorname{PD}(S)\\), with\n\\[\n\\Delta c^2=-4,\\quad \\Delta c_2=-1,\\quad\\Delta\\kappa=0.\n\\]\nIts order-two boundary character is returned to the fixed trace representative by the same determinant-preserving conjugation. Intrinsic tensor-related roots preserve the normalized sphere test. Proposition 16’s cap points and full framed deformation-and-cut complexes correspond.</p><p>Use the same ordered cap lines \\(K_e\\), gluing maps \\(\\Gamma_e\\), comparison sign \\(s\\), and weights \\(\\varepsilon_1=-s\\varepsilon_0\\) as in Lemma 4.2. All toral, Weyl, framing, mark, and target signs are already included. Connected configuration sectors and orientability make the comparison constant; fixed minimum charge excludes an internal degree change.</p><p>Homotope the spinor deformation operator to the real instanton operator plus the coupled complex Dirac operator. Tensoring and boundary conjugation act complex-linearly on the latter, giving sign \\(+1\\). Exterior phase and cut factors agree. Thus no new sign choice occurs. The exterior branch lists are paired individually, and Proposition 16 preserves their weights.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 17.3", "orig": "Completion of Theorem 9.1 in Section 10.4", "depth": 1, "snippet": "The free interior can be regularized relative to the instanton and lens collars, while preserving \\(\\Omega\\), all forbidden-stratum exclusions, and paired lens data.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The free interior can be regularized relative to the instanton and lens collars, while preserving \\(\\Omega\\), all forbidden-stratum exclusions, and paired lens data. The resulting collared weighted one-dimensional space has no further ideal boundary.</p>", "proof": "<p>After Proposition 16, use small single-valued variations supported off the protected collars. Its one \\(P\\)-component gives full universal surjectivity, and expected dimension is one. Apply the relative generic construction on compact remainders, or a countable library. The compact candidate argument of Proposition 15 preserves every completed exclusion under sufficiently small changes.</p><p>Propositions 12–15 leave only legitimate instantons and single minimum trace ends. Body and true-neck compactness from Proposition 13 and exhaustion from Proposition 16 show that adjoining those collars gives the compact weighted object. All choices stay in the permitted ordinary neighborhood, preserving \\(\\Omega\\).</p>"}], "edges": [[1, 0, "cite"], [2, 0, "cite"], [2, 13, "cite"], [2, 16, "cite"], [2, 17, "cite"], [2, 18, "cite"], [2, 20, "cite"], [2, 22, "cite"], [2, 24, "cite"], [2, 25, "cite"], [2, 31, "cite"], [2, 49, "cite"], [2, 51, "cite"], [2, 53, "cite"], [2, 60, "cite"], [3, 2, "cite"], [4, 2, "cite"], [5, 2, "cite"], [5, 6, "cite"], [6, 2, "cite"], [7, 2, "cite"], [8, 2, "cite"], [9, 2, "cite"], [10, 2, "cite"], [11, 2, "cite"], [12, 2, "cite"], [13, 0, "cite"], [13, 50, "cite"], [14, 13, "part"], [15, 13, "part"], [16, 13, "part"], [17, 13, "part"], [18, 13, "part"], [19, 0, "cite"], [19, 52, "cite"], [19, 53, "cite"], [20, 19, "cite"], [21, 19, "cite"], [21, 22, "cite"], [21, 90, "cite"], [22, 19, "cite"], [22, 25, "cite"], [23, 19, "cite"], [23, 24, "cite"], [24, 19, "cite"], [24, 25, "cite"], [25, 19, "cite"], [26, 0, "cite"], [26, 49, "cite"], [27, 26, "part"], [28, 26, "cite"], [29, 26, "cite"], [30, 26, "cite"], [31, 26, "cite"], [31, 48, "cite"], [31, 49, "cite"], [32, 0, "cite"], [32, 36, "cite"], [32, 52, "cite"], [33, 32, "cite"], [34, 32, "cite"], [35, 32, "cite"], [36, 0, "cite"], [36, 45, "cite"], [36, 52, "cite"], [37, 36, "cite"], [38, 36, "cite"], [39, 36, "cite"], [40, 36, "cite"], [41, 36, "cite"], [42, 0, "cite"], [42, 48, "cite"], [42, 51, "cite"], [42, 52, "cite"], [42, 55, "cite"], [42, 62, "cite"], [42, 64, "cite"], [43, 42, "cite"], [44, 42, "cite"], [44, 45, "cite"], [44, 70, "cite"], [45, 42, "cite"], [45, 46, "cite"], [46, 42, "cite"], [46, 47, "cite"], [47, 42, "cite"], [48, 0, "cite"], [48, 52, "cite"], [48, 55, "cite"], [49, 48, "cite"], [50, 48, "cite"], [51, 48, "cite"], [52, 0, "cite"], [52, 60, "cite"], [52, 67, "cite"], [52, 88, "cite"], [53, 52, "part"], [54, 55, "cite"], [55, 52, "cite"], [56, 52, "cite"], [57, 0, "cite"], [57, 61, "cite"], [57, 66, "cite"], [57, 75, "cite"], [57, 83, "cite"], [58, 57, "cite"], [59, 57, "cite"], [60, 0, "cite"], [60, 72, "cite"], [60, 74, "cite"], [60, 75, "cite"], [60, 78, "cite"], [60, 80, "cite"], [60, 91, "cite"], [61, 72, "cite"], [62, 65, "cite"], [63, 60, "part"], [64, 60, "cite"], [65, 60, "cite"], [65, 66, "cite"], [66, 60, "cite"], [67, 0, "cite"], [67, 75, "cite"], [67, 78, "cite"], [67, 79, "cite"], [67, 80, "cite"], [67, 87, "cite"], [67, 91, "cite"], [68, 67, "cite"], [68, 69, "cite"], [69, 67, "cite"], [70, 67, "cite"], [70, 73, "cite"], [71, 67, "cite"], [71, 73, "cite"], [72, 67, "cite"], [73, 67, "cite"], [74, 0, "cite"], [74, 78, "cite"], [74, 88, "cite"], [74, 91, "cite"], [75, 74, "cite"], [76, 74, "cite"], [77, 74, "cite"], [78, 0, "cite"], [78, 82, "cite"], [78, 88, "cite"], [78, 91, "cite"], [79, 78, "cite"], [80, 78, "cite"], [81, 78, "cite"], [82, 0, "cite"], [82, 88, "cite"], [82, 90, "cite"], [82, 91, "cite"], [83, 82, "cite"], [84, 82, "cite"], [85, 82, "cite"], [85, 86, "cite"], [86, 82, "cite"], [86, 87, "cite"], [87, 82, "cite"], [88, 0, "cite"], [89, 88, "cite"], [90, 88, "cite"], [91, 88, "cite"]]});
